{"id": "effect-95a2b64f-bae1-5cc1-b9ce-bf6b6fd383a7", "sessionId": "0c772a71-d421-e6a8-14d9-dcb034248b0b", "turnId": "turn-2f430b3805d99f342897b43671b5e559", "createdAt": 1792406769019, "updatedAt": 1792406769094, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "effect", "title": "file_system.read_file", "detail": {"toolName": "file_system.read_file", "display": {"summary": "Reading C:/Users/AudunArnesenNyhus/.codex/AGENTS.md", "content": null, "suffix": "", "verb": "Reading", "message": "C:/Users/AudunArnesenNyhus/.codex/AGENTS.md", "settledVerb": "Read", "settledMessage": "C:/Users/AudunArnesenNyhus/.codex/AGENTS.md", "statusText": "Reading file"}, "kind": "file_read", "input": {"filePath": "C:/Users/AudunArnesenNyhus/.codex/AGENTS.md", "offset": null, "limit": 2000}}, "state": {"status": "completed", "output": {"filePath": "C:\\Users\\AudunArnesenNyhus\\.codex\\AGENTS.md", "content": "# Working preferences\n\n- Complete authorised implementation, verification and handoff. Make reasonable reversible choices; ask only when missing information changes correctness, scope or external consequences. Investigation alone does not authorise fixes. Existing approval remains valid within its scope, including routine disposable checks and their repair.\n- Follow the current request and repository contract. Historical plans are evidence, not active authority. Keep repository-specific engineering, qualification, resource and release rules in their repositories; keep shared worker policy here. Global guidance and skills must not weaken repository gates, add unrelated work or reuse consumed authority.\n- Read only needed symbols, ranges and guidance; batch independent reads. Keep full diagnostics on disk and report counts, failures and paths. Estimate broad output before printing; avoid full catalogs, large arrays and encoded media. Read `~/.codex/harness/README.md` only when its optional helpers are needed.\n- Verify proportionately and meet required gates. Check effective runtime settings before claiming a configuration works. Repeat passed checks only for changed inputs, failures or unresolved concerns. Preserve failed evidence, scientific tolerances and independent reviews; distinguish implementation, testing, acceptance and release. Never claim unmeasured savings.\n- Use bounded waits and compact status checks; checkpoint work spanning turns. On pause, launch no new work, stop only owned activity as requested and save minimum restart state. Create user-visible tasks only when requested.\n- Lead with the result in concise plain language. Report meaningful changes, evidence and blockers; avoid repeated status and ritual approvals.\n\n# Model roles and ownership\n\nAstra uses the configured OpenAI model and owns decomposition, repository selection, architecture and contract decisions, worker authority, verification, reconciliation, integration, Git delivery and final acceptance. Honour explicit model/effort choices.\n\n| Level | Default responsibility |\n|---|---|\n| Local OpenCode | Short, tightly specified lookups, evidence extraction, documentation, mechanical edits and simple checks within demonstrated capability. |\n| Mistral | Default owner of substantial bounded implementation milestones: investigation, candidate design, central algorithm, connected integration, debugging, focused verification, repair, documentation and handoff. Independent review of other authors when useful. |\n| OpenAI | Orchestration, architecture, governing contracts, unresolved high-consequence reasoning, scientific adjudication, cross-repository integration and final acceptance; direct implementation when coupling, confidentiality or availability warrants it. |\n\nBounded multiagent workflows are welcome when specialization, implementation capacity or independent review adds value. Astra may delegate autonomously within authorised scope. Use the lowest suitable level; dispatch suitable substantial work directly to Mistral without a prerequisite local attempt. Avoid Mistral overhead for trivial work, duplicate investigations, overlapping writes, full-history forks and delegation merely to create activity. Keep tightly coupled work with one owner; independent work may proceed concurrently within shared resource limits.\n\nFor substantial implementation, assign Mistral the largest coherent outcome fitting established contracts and budgets, including its central algorithm and integration. Do not routinely keep coding with Astra while assigning Mistral only helper work, tests or documentation. Numerical difficulty alone does not reserve implementation for Astra. Mistral may inspect and plan the route, propose alternatives, derive candidate equations, choose compatible internal algorithms/data layouts, simplify duplicate services, build verification tools and repair its owned code and harness.\n\nThis applies across the ANY ecosystem to complete solver, mechanics, meshing, geometry, application and tooling subsystems: static/transient and nonlinear routes, enforcement/contact/history, materials/elements, assembly and operators, retained-coordinate/dynamics mechanics, geometry/event control, state/restart/rollback, public APIs/persistence/results, shared-service refactoring, benchmark/reference tooling, CI/build/install workflows and measured performance/correctness repair. Repository ownership and execution authority remain explicit; eligibility grants no sibling edits, qualification campaigns or publication.\n\nAstra supplies governing contracts and adopts changes to physical assumptions, public compatibility, architecture boundaries, scientific tolerances, capability claims or execution authority. Workers may propose changes with evidence but may not adopt reserved changes unilaterally. Resolve concrete boundaries with a concise delta and let the same owner finish. Prefer useful delivered outcomes over worker quotas or approval ceremonies. If Astra implements directly while Mistral is available, briefly record the concrete coupling, confidentiality or reserved-decision reason in the existing task note. Apply ownership changes prospectively; do not restart completed work to change its author.\n\n# Assignment, communication and completion\n\n- Determine the repository from the request, checkout, remotes and project context; pass its absolute root. Read permits investigation, planning or review without edits; Edit permits expressly authorised implementation, tests or documentation.\n- Before Edit dispatch, inspect enough context to preserve dirty work and concurrent ownership. Give one outcome-oriented brief: repository, mode, owned files/subsystem, objective, essential context, governing contracts and task-specific invariants, protected evidence, shared resource limits, permitted checks and acceptance/handoff criteria. Allow new files only within the owned boundary; cross-repository work needs separately authorised ownership.\n- Authorise the connected inspect/design/implement/debug/check/repair/document loop within that boundary. Permit ordinary reversible decisions, impact-based check selection and repair of permitted failures without serial permission requests. Escalate concrete contract, ownership, authority or resource boundaries. Workers must not launch workers, broaden ownership or consume additional authority. Delegation never multiplies numerical budgets or renews frozen authority.\n- Workers must not commit, push, change branches/history, reset/discard work, edit other repositories or alter unrelated configuration unless Astra explicitly grants that authority. Astra owns Git integration and publication.\n- Keep coordination compact: send only scope, objective, authority, essential context and criteria; reference artifacts instead of repeating history/logs. Batch related messages, use deltas on follow-up and avoid routine acknowledgements/status. Reports contain outcome, changed files, checks actually run, actionable findings, blockers and evidence paths. Keep full diagnostics on disk; preserve uncertainty and failed evidence.\n- For substantial Edit assignments, include one early progress checkpoint before inspection consumes most of the turn budget: concrete findings, intended edit, remaining work and blockers. Use a compact artifact or report, not a permission gate. Set a checkpoint deadline appropriate to the assignment. At that point Astra inspects actual artifacts and remaining implementation/check/handoff work using a compact status read, not repeated polling. If the checkpoint is missing or investigation dominates, inspect the cause and supply missing context, narrow the deliverable or stop a demonstrably stalled owned run; do not wait blindly for the turn limit. Let the owner choose internal sequencing and compatible methods within its scope.\r\n- Give every Mistral Edit assignment a realistic turn budget and explicit completion requirements. Reserve the final turns for focused verification and handoff. Before exhausting the limit, save a concise checkpoint at an explicitly permitted evidence path or designated task note (use the final response if file permissions prevent saving): changed files, completed checks, failures, remaining work, unresolved decisions and evidence paths. Label handoff state as inspection only, partial implementation, implemented but unverified, or verified against assigned criteria, with evidence and remaining gaps. Worker verification does not establish final acceptance; never claim checks that did not run.\n- For resource-bounded checks, use a small execution harness that records the exact command, input/candidate identity, effective runtime, exit or timeout status, precise process-wall duration, cumulative budget used and remaining, counts, full stdout/stderr and machine-readable results when supported. Use unique evidence paths for each attempt; preserve failures and partial timeout output. Bound each process-tree timeout by the remaining authorised check budget, retaining any integration/verification reserve. Do not substitute pytest-reported duration or rounded shell seconds for required process-wall accounting. If accounting is missing, recover existing evidence and report uncertainty; do not rerun to reconstruct it or assume authority remains. This adds no numerical execution authority or acceptance gate.\r\n- A limit-reached run or missing handoff is incomplete unless inspected evidence satisfies the task. Inspect partial artifacts and remaining execution authority before a focused continuation; return actionable findings to the same owner with a delta brief when available. If required verification cannot fit the remaining budget, preserve the candidate and report the unmet gate; do not waive checks or renew authority. Continue the same owner from useful artifacts with missing context or actionable findings; a turn limit alone does not justify takeover. Astra takes over when execution is unreliable, the owner is unavailable, or a concrete coupling, confidentiality or reserved-decision boundary requires it; record the reason briefly. Narrow work after repeated unfinished runs; extend turn limits only for demonstrated progress within remaining authority, preserving prior failures and required checks. Classify service failure, intentional interruption, turn exhaustion, implementation defects and verification-evidence gaps separately; absent checks are not disobedience when execution was prohibited. If interrupted before checkpointing, Astra preserves the logs and reconstructs actual progress. Limits are execution bounds, not guaranteed token caps.\n\n# Worker entry points\n\nUse the wrappers (PowerShell 7):\n\n```powershell\n& \"$HOME\\bin\\mistral-worker.ps1\" `\n    -Repo \"<absolute repository root>\" -Mode <Read|Edit> `\n    -Task \"<bounded outcome, ownership and contracts>\" -MaxTurns <N>\n\n& \"$HOME\\bin\\opencode-worker.ps1\" `\n    -Repo \"<absolute repository root>\" -Mode <Read|Edit> `\n    -Task \"<bounded task>\" -MaxSteps <N> -TimeoutSeconds <N>\n```\n\nOmit local `-Model` for the configured default, or supply `-Model \"<configured local provider/model>\"`; do not hard-code today's model into policy. Do not download models, reconfigure the server or switch local-only data to cloud inference without authorisation. Follow each wrapper's actual permission contract.\n\nThe local wrapper supports configured OpenAI-compatible loopback HTTP(S) providers, pins worker/helper inference to the selected model and disables sharing/external plugins. Its application permissions are not an OS sandbox or complete network isolation. Read denies edits; both modes deny unlisted tools/commands, nested workers, network tools and external directories except OpenCode's internal tool-output directory. Environment-file protections remain intact; Edit permits only owned repository edits.\n\nThe default shell allowlist contains specific Git inspections. Add `-AllowedCommands @('<exact authorised command>')` only for needed Read inspections or relevant Edit checks; never bypass failures with arbitrary interpreters, shell composition, wildcards or Git integration commands. Logs stay outside repositories; local `-OutputDirectory` selects a new evidence directory.\n\nQueue short local jobs through the wrapper's shared local-server slot. `-QueueWaitSeconds` bounds waiting separately (default 15 seconds; 0 for immediate fallback); `-TimeoutSeconds` starts when the process launches after acquiring the slot. `LOCAL_QUEUE_BUSY` means no inference started: use suitable Mistral/OpenAI when waiting would delay delivery, respecting local-only constraints, or defer. Queue records preserve wait/execution timing. This coordinates cooperating wrappers, not direct Ollama clients; do not bypass the queue or repeatedly retry a busy slot. Prefer short local jobs over long audits. Use proportionate Mistral `MaxTurns`, local agent `MaxSteps` and local process-tree `TimeoutSeconds`.\n\n# Availability and fallback\n\nLocal models and Mistral are optional dependencies. Local services/models/hardware/tools may be unavailable or unreliable; Mistral may have startup, authentication, transport, context, inference, rate-limit or allowance failures. Treat exhausted Mistral usage as unavailable for the current task.\n\nCheck availability only when useful, using one small bounded Read task through the wrapper and inspecting successful tool outcomes. A launcher, running service, CLI version, plausible answer or zero exit code alone is insufficient. Reuse established availability; do not probe before every assignment. Retain cached unavailability/exhaustion for the current task until new evidence warrants a useful attempt; do not repeatedly retry the same failure or probe unrelated workers.\n\nIf local is unavailable, use suitable Mistral/OpenAI; if Mistral is unavailable, use OpenAI; if both are unavailable, Astra continues directly. Preserve useful partial work and failed evidence. Optional workers must not block delivery or silently relax local-only constraints. Report availability when it affects the requested workflow, confidence, scope or timing. Identify any required local or independent gate left unmet; fallback does not establish different-family independence.\n\n# Verification and acceptance\n\nAfter every Edit worker, inspect Git status/diff, confirm ownership and preserved dirty work, independently run or inspect relevant checks, and resolve unexpected changes or failures. For behavior-changing repairs, confirm regression coverage of the affected contracts, including refusal, mutation and cancellation paths where relevant; add focused tests when existing coverage is insufficient. Helpers alone do not prove caller or production-path integration. Require evidence for important test-pass claims. Check consequential Read findings against source, data, equations or runtime state. For local runs, inspect effective model routing, permissions/steps and actual tool outcomes in saved logs.\n\nFor useful independent review of consequential OpenAI implementation, numerics, mechanics contracts or difficult debugging, prefer an available Mistral Read reviewer; a suitable local model may provide supporting independent input. Prefer different model families; OpenCode is a runner, not a family. Give the artifact and question without Astra's preferred answer unless that answer is under review. Seek errors, counterexamples, unsupported assumptions and missing tests; review complete slices with evidence rather than redundant/trivial fragments. An implementation author cannot supply its sole required independent assessment.\n\nResolve disagreement through underlying evidence and a discriminating check when practical. Preserve scientific gates, physical assumptions, tolerances, failed evidence, consumed authority, release restrictions and unresolved uncertainty. Worker reports and model agreement are evidence, not acceptance; Astra owns final acceptance and user-facing handoff.\r\n", "numLines": 76, "startLine": 1, "requestedOffset": null, "requestedLimit": 2000, "totalLines": null, "wasTruncated": false}, "outputText": "", "durationMs": 0.0, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Read", "message": "76 lines from C:\\Users\\AudunArnesenNyhus\\.codex\\AGENTS.md", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": ""}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
{"id": "effect-f4228d19-5bee-5b7f-90dc-befc5549cbb6", "sessionId": "0c772a71-d421-e6a8-14d9-dcb034248b0b", "turnId": "turn-2f430b3805d99f342897b43671b5e559", "createdAt": 1792406769026, "updatedAt": 1792406769122, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "effect", "title": "file_system.read_file", "detail": {"toolName": "file_system.read_file", "display": {"summary": "Reading C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\ECOSYSTEM_GUIDE.md", "content": null, "suffix": "", "verb": "Reading", "message": "C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\ECOSYSTEM_GUIDE.md", "settledVerb": "Read", "settledMessage": "C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\ECOSYSTEM_GUIDE.md", "statusText": "Reading file"}, "kind": "file_read", "input": {"filePath": "C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\ECOSYSTEM_GUIDE.md", "offset": null, "limit": 2000}}, "state": {"status": "completed", "output": {"filePath": "C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\ECOSYSTEM_GUIDE.md", "content": "# ANY Ecosystem Guide\r\n\r\n> **Purpose:** Keep every ANY contribution correct, interoperable, traceable and\r\n> releasable—regardless of whether it is written by a person or an AI assistant.\r\n\r\n- **Applies to:** every contributor and every ANY repository.\r\n- **Canonical source:** `ANYopenSoft/ECOSYSTEM_GUIDE.md`.\r\n- **Local rule:** repository documentation may add constraints, but may not\r\n  weaken applicable technical or release requirements. Keep the policy reference\r\n  current; preserve explicitly adopted repository-specific requirements.\r\n\r\n## Risk-proportionate development — effective 2026-09-24\r\n\r\nApply policy `ANY_ECOSYSTEM_RISK_PROPORTIONATE_ENGINEERING_V1`, revision\r\n`2026-09-24.2`, in the canonical [ANY ecosystem philosophy](https://github.com/audunarn/ANYopenSoft/blob/main/governance/ECOSYSTEM_PHILOSOPHY.md).\r\nFor this workspace the effective adopted file is\r\n`C:/Github/ANYopenSoft/governance/ECOSYSTEM_PHILOSOPHY.md`; use it until this\r\nrevision is published. Do not assume the remote page already contains local edits.\r\n\r\nAssess consequence, uncertainty, exposure and intended claim. Keep one short\r\nliving task record. Level A permits authorized integrated development experiments\r\nwith focused checks and early scrutiny of high-risk assumptions. Level B requires\r\nprospective criteria, exact identity, retained evidence and independent acceptance.\r\nLevel C retains applicable release/installed-artifact requirements. A low-risk patch\r\ndoes not need an artificial scientific acceptance study. Reuse valid evidence by\r\nimpact analysis; do not require a separate dossier or fixed reviewer count per helper.\r\n\r\nThis replaces blanket future administrative ceremony, not scientific tolerances,\r\nfrozen registrations, consumed run authority, explicit no-run instructions,\r\nprotected evidence or release requirements. Active task owners record a short\r\nprospective transition in their existing note, retain substantive gates, and obtain\r\nany contract-required amendment before changing procedure. Resource scheduling\r\nfollows the effective workspace policy, including `C:/Github/AGENTS.md` locally.\r\n\r\n## Non-negotiables\r\n\r\n| Priority | Rule |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| **Ownership** | Domain truth lives in its canonical repository and is consumed through public contracts. |\r\n| **Correctness** | Sound theory and independently verifiable results outrank performance. |\r\n| **Safety** | Ambiguity, unsupported input and incomplete evidence fail closed. |\r\n| **Traceability** | Claims bind exact source, inputs, environment, commands and artifacts. |\r\n| **Compatibility** | Breaking changes require an explicit decision and migration path. |\r\n| **Delivery** | Required tests and CI are green before merge or release. |\r\n\r\n## 1. Structure and ownership\r\n\r\n| Area | Canonical owner |\r\n|---|---|\r\n| Geometry, topology, identity, tolerances, spatial/intersection truth | `ANYgeometry` |\r\n| Discretization and mesh generation | `ANYmesh` (distribution: `ANYmesher`) |\r\n| Material definitions and material provenance | `ANYmaterial` |\r\n| Interchange and file semantics | `ANYfileIO` / `ANYio` during their migration |\r\n| Optional OCCT interchange backend | `ANYfileio-occt` |\r\n| Solver mathematics, elements, assembly and numerical results | `ANYsolver` |\r\n| Prescriptive and semi-analytical buckling rules | `ANYbuckling` |\r\n| FEM projects, jobs, orchestration and result artifacts | `ANYfem` |\r\n| Engineering application consuming the public owners above | `ANYstructure` |\r\n| Backend-neutral 3D scene/view contracts | `ANY3dView` |\r\n| Tk presentation adapter | `ANYtk3D` |\r\n| Time-series processing | `ANYtimeseries` |\r\n| Intelligence/automation consumers; no duplicated engineering truth | `ANYintelligent` |\r\n| Governance, ecosystem documentation and public coordination | `ANYopenSoft` |\r\n| Product/workspace orchestration; no duplicated domain truth | `ANYworkspaceAI` |\r\n| Experimental/reserved repositories | `ANYtrade`, `anyfea3d` |\r\n\r\nDependencies point from owners to consumers. A consumer must use a public owner\r\ncontract, not copy its algorithms, constants, schemas or data. During the\r\n`ANYfileIO`/`ANYio` transition, each task must name one authoritative line and\r\nprovide an explicit compatibility path. Headless contracts precede GUI work. SI\r\nunits and provenance are required at repository boundaries.\r\n\r\n### Engineering doctrine\r\n\r\n- Correctness and sound theory outrank speed.\r\n- Hidden corrections, empirical gate-tuning, invented stiffness and silent\r\n  fallback are prohibited.\r\n- Ambiguity fails closed with a typed, actionable error.\r\n\r\n## 2. Required development practice\r\n\r\n1. Define the intended behavior, owner, compatibility impact and acceptance\r\n   evidence before nontrivial implementation.\r\n2. Keep changes narrow. Do not mix unrelated cleanup, formatting or capability\r\n   expansion into the same change.\r\n3. Treat AI output as untrusted input. A responsible contributor reviews every\r\n   line, verifies licenses and provenance, and runs the same gates required for\r\n   human-written work. Never provide secrets, customer data or restricted\r\n   material to an unapproved model or service.\r\n4. Preserve public compatibility. Breaking changes require an explicit ecosystem\r\n   decision, migration path and later version-policy change; they cannot ship as\r\n   a patch.\r\n5. Never weaken a test, tolerance or validation rule merely to obtain a pass.\r\n\r\n## 3. Testing gates\r\n\r\n> **Merge rule:** all applicable gates must pass from a clean checkout.\r\n> Select development checks by risk and impact; full release requirements remain\r\n> release gates, not prerequisites for every Level-A experiment.\r\n\r\n- Every behavior change has focused unit tests; every defect fix has a regression\r\n  that fails without the fix.\r\n- Public interfaces have contract tests. Cross-repository changes test both owner\r\n  and consumer against declared supported versions.\r\n- Numerical and physics changes include independent analytical, published or\r\n  separately implemented references. Comparing code with itself is not\r\n  qualification.\r\n- Physics tests cover units, signs, frames, invariance, conservation/equilibrium,\r\n  limiting cases, convergence and invalid inputs as applicable. Tolerances must\r\n  follow theory or quantified numerical error, never observed output alone.\r\n- Failure, cancellation, timeout, stale-data and fallback paths are tested.\r\n  Fallback is permitted only when explicit, safe and reported.\r\n- For supported delivery/release and changes affecting installed behavior,\r\n  distributions are built and checked; an installed wheel is imported and\r\n  smoke-tested outside the source tree. GUI/executable changes also receive a\r\n  packaged-artifact smoke test on each claimed platform.\r\n- Supported Python and operating-system claims are exercised in CI. A platform or\r\n  version may be removed only by an explicit compatibility decision.\r\n- Skips and expected failures require a recorded reason, owner and removal\r\n  condition. Flaky tests are defects, not acceptable retries.\r\n- Required CI must be green. A red, missing or cancelled gate is not evidence.\r\n\r\nDocumentation-only changes may use documentation/format checks when they cannot\r\naffect runtime, packaging, commands or public contracts.\r\n\r\n## 4. Qualification and traceability\r\n\r\nEvery qualification claim must identify:\r\n\r\n- requirement, issue or decision and the exact qualified scope;\r\n- repository, commit, tree, package version and dependency versions;\r\n- commands, toolchain, Python, OS and relevant hardware;\r\n- input identities, units, origins, licenses and SHA-256 hashes where practical;\r\n- test and benchmark outputs, tolerances, limitations and failed/omitted cases;\r\n- produced artifact identities and the reviewer/approver.\r\n\r\nEvidence is immutable or content-addressed and reproducible from recorded inputs.\r\nGenerated, transformed and experimental data retain lineage. Standards, material\r\ntables, reference results and third-party models require edition/source and\r\nusage-rights records. Performance evidence never substitutes for correctness.\r\nClaims must not exceed the executed matrix or reference quality.\r\n\r\n## 5. Version and release policy\r\n\r\nVersions use `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`.\r\n\r\n> **Current release rule:** increment **PATCH only** until this policy is\r\n> explicitly replaced.\r\n\r\n- repository releases increment `A.B.X -> A.B.(X+1)`;\r\n- major and minor increments are reserved and require a new ecosystem decision;\r\n- patch releases must remain backward compatible; incompatible work is deferred\r\n  or protected by a compatible migration/shim;\r\n- package metadata, runtime `__version__`, tests, manifests and release\r\n  documentation must agree;\r\n- tags are `vA.B.X` and must match the built artifact version exactly;\r\n- schemas and file formats carry their own versions and backward-read/migration\r\n  rules; a package bump does not silently change a schema;\r\n- PyPI publication always uses GitHub Actions OIDC Trusted Publishing. API tokens,\r\n  passwords and manual credential uploads are prohibited.\r\n\r\nA version bump does not prove qualification. Release requires the testing and\r\ntraceability gates above, reviewed artifacts, and explicit publication authority.\r\n\r\nDecision-oriented investigation: before a substantial diagnostic batch, use the\r\nexisting living record to identify the question, competing explanations, bounded\r\nexperiment and how outcomes change the next action. Tie refinements, sweeps and\r\nnew reference tools to the principal uncertainty; interpret the combined evidence\r\nand choose the next action rather than automatically repeating at greater cost.\r\nThis adds no registration, approval or reporting gate. Scientific acceptance\r\ncriteria and authority/resource limits remain unchanged; a spent diagnostic budget\r\ndoes not prove formulation failure. See the canonical philosophy's\r\n\"Decision-oriented investigation\" section.\r\n", "numLines": 173, "startLine": 1, "requestedOffset": null, "requestedLimit": 2000, "totalLines": null, "wasTruncated": false}, "outputText": "", "durationMs": 0.0, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Read", "message": "173 lines from C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\ECOSYSTEM_GUIDE.md", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": ""}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
{"id": "effect-dd6e2234-e93e-5bfa-97ec-eeb5abeb86eb", "sessionId": "0c772a71-d421-e6a8-14d9-dcb034248b0b", "turnId": "turn-2f430b3805d99f342897b43671b5e559", "createdAt": 1792406769033, "updatedAt": 1792406769137, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "effect", "title": "file_system.read_file", "detail": {"toolName": "file_system.read_file", "display": {"summary": "Reading C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\docs\\BARGE_VIEW_PERFORMANCE.md", "content": null, "suffix": "", "verb": "Reading", "message": "C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\docs\\BARGE_VIEW_PERFORMANCE.md", "settledVerb": "Read", "settledMessage": "C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\docs\\BARGE_VIEW_PERFORMANCE.md", "statusText": "Reading file"}, "kind": "file_read", "input": {"filePath": "C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\docs\\BARGE_VIEW_PERFORMANCE.md", "offset": null, "limit": 2000}}, "state": {"status": "completed", "output": {"filePath": "C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\docs\\BARGE_VIEW_PERFORMANCE.md", "content": "# Barge viewport performance — 2026-10-05\n\nUser authorizes investigation and fixes for camera manipulation of the Examples-menu floating barge, particularly meshed. Viewer base 7335aec. Preserve scientific owner topology, selection identities, clipping/deformation, semantic outlines and all unrelated dirty work. No release or numerical qualification campaign.\n\nQuestion: is latency caused by backend fallback, CPU per-primitive camera work, GPU uploads/draw calls, or application rebuilds? Measure actual Qt backend, camera frames and profile stacks on the example geometry and a bounded representative display mesh. Do not infer engineering mesh acceptance from display fixtures. Compare identical scenes/cameras before and after; test preserved visual and picking contracts. Original saved mesh settings are unknown; user confirms built-in example.\n\nMistral owns the connected renderer implementation in the isolated viewer worktree (Qt/retained/GPU and focused tests/tooling), with no Git delivery or sibling edits. Astra owns ANYfem runtime reproduction, contracts, source review, baseline/candidate comparison and local integration. Existing geometry adapter edits are excluded.\r\n\nParent initial reproduction: actual ANYfem Examples-menu geometry has 4522 triangles; uniform 0.25 m display-only shell refinement gives 9712 triangles, retained beam geometry. Automatic backend activates GPU with no diagnostics. Initial input-tick benchmark (includes explicit repaint + processEvents, so software draws twice) gives ~17/19 ms GPU geometry/mesh, ~1.4/2.9 s software. Software profile: repeated project_point/basis and outline projection dominate. Separate ANYfem worker owns a demonstrated workbench refresh feedback loop (~17ms tree/panels every idle tick). Parent will rerun normalized one-paint comparisons after workers stop; do not touch application code or infer performance ratios from these provisional timings.\r\n\nApplication owner found unchanged ResultsTask._set_frame_index publishes shape_index on every panel refresh. Parent review rejected broad suppression of events during refresh; owner removed it. Two bounded worker attempts ended without regression files, so Astra completed independent verification: baseline idle test fails with 10 refreshes/10 ticks; candidate five focused tests pass (idle quiescence, meaningful frame/clamp updates, reentrant event delivery, edit/undo/selection). Only tasks.py requires production change. No worker Git changes.\n\nConcurrent owner changed live ANYfem examples.py/solve/build.py during measurements. Preserve those files; freeze fd5faf2 in .worktrees/barge-performance-baseline-20261005 and compare against the application candidate at the same base. Initial timings using live source are exploratory, not acceptance. Parent benchmark corrected explicit repaint+processEvents duplication to one event-loop update; final timing claims use matching frozen scenes.\r\n\n## Delivered local fixes and evidence\n\nMistral implemented the frame-local vertex projection and scalar-range hoisting. Its final bounded outline assignment ended in analysis without an edit; after three useful but incomplete renderer runs, Astra completed the narrowly specified no-clipping outline projection batch and independent parity test. No topology cache or reduced-detail rendering was introduced. Camera/section clipping falls back to the existing algorithm; the fast path uses the same semantic loops, including actual element boundaries.\n\nANYfem's result-frame setter now publishes only actual clamped frame changes. This eliminates the demonstrated idle feedback loop without discarding events delivered during a refresh. The local application and viewer source checkouts contain the fixes, uncommitted; unrelated edits were preserved. The example owner independently committed its concurrent changes; the final local integration test also passes on that current checkout.\n\n### Controlled measurements\n\nNative Windows Python 3.14.2 / PySide6 6.10.1. Six camera-input ticks per case, identical frozen ANYfem fd5faf2 example, viewer base 7335aec versus candidate. Default Automatic selected GPU with empty diagnostics. Uniform shell quads at 0.7 m are a display fixture, not an engineering mesh or scientific acceptance test; beam geometry retained. Results are local measurements, not universal frame-rate guarantees.\n\n| Scene/backend | Baseline median ms | Candidate median ms |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Example geometry / GPU | 24.12 | 16.60 |\n| Display mesh (5,280 triangles) / GPU | 21.32 | 16.57 |\n| Example geometry / software | 701.80 | 240.30 |\n| Display mesh (5,280 triangles) / software | 801.02 | 272.38 |\n| Separate 20,000-triangle display fixture / software | 2915.95 | 1066.89 |\n\nAll four matching example captures (geometry/mesh, GPU/software) are pixel-identical before/after. Software improves substantially but remains unsuitable for fluid dense-scene interaction; native GPU remains the appropriate interactive route. No scientific tests, tolerances, solver/mesher pins or geometry were changed for this fix.\n\n### Verification\n\n- Baseline application idle regression fails: 10 refreshes in 10 idle ticks. Candidate is quiescent; real changes and reentrant events still delivered.\n- Viewer software/shared contracts: 62 passed, 9 explicitly GPU-opt-in skips; subsequent native Qt/GPU run: all 23 passed, zero skips.\n- Independent projection/semantic-edge oracle: 8 passed, including strict depth boundaries, viewport/camera changes and framebuffer scaling. Unclipped edge batch matches original segment projection.\n- Application focused integration: 5 passed (idle, frame/clamp, reentrant updates, edit/undo/selection, selection routes). Repeated after local integration/current example: 5 passed.\n\nEvidence: C:/Github/ANYfem/reports/qt/barge-performance/frozen-{baseline,candidate}-{auto,software}/ (JSON, profiles, captures); local-integration-tests.log in the same parent. Renderer evidence: C:/Github/ANY3dView/.worktrees/barge-view-performance-20261005/benchmark-results/ (test logs and camera-performance/qt-camera-software-verified-{baseline,candidate}.json). Earlier exploratory results and failed evidence remain retained; only controlled runs above support the timing claims.\n\nRestart ANYfem to reload the modified Python modules. No package, commit, push or PR was published for this task. Preserved worktrees retain the candidate and frozen reproduction sources.\r\n\n## 2026-10-14 delegated Qt latency investigation\n\nScope: renderer/presentation only, preserve all current dirty edits; no ANYfem edits or scientific runs. Captured 45s profile shows ~1s Python paint and ~0.65s poll callbacks, insufficient to establish reported global lag. Question: does native composition/vsync delay the whole window despite short Python paint callbacks? Bounded experiment compares requested swap interval 1/0 using actual unmeshed example, four seconds of 16ms camera updates each, tagging actual renderer submissions and frameSwapped. Acceptance: effective setting changes and materially reduced matched request-to-swap delay; otherwise do not change product defaults. GUI budget inherited: 73.4644437s remaining; harness records precise wall time and receipts. Shared existing renderer edits and tight presentation experiment stay under this owner; no nested worker dispatch. New policy applied prospectively, prior evidence preserved.\r\n\n### Presentation checkpoint / handoff, 2026-10-14\n\nState: inspection and bounded experiment completed; no product fix justified or applied. Both probes used the live unmeshed ANYfem example and current dirty viewer source. Native GPU, effective viewport 1860x811 (different from the reported 2560x1369); synthetic Qt timer camera updates, not native mouse/button UX acceptance.\n\n- Requested swap 1: context/window interval 1/1; 243 updates, 241 matched frames; median request-to-swap 16.3411ms, p95 16.9031ms, max17.8075ms; median renderer CPU1.0632ms.\n- Requested swap 0: context/window interval1/0; 246 updates, 242 matched frames; median16.37345ms, p95 16.829175ms, max17.3159ms; median renderer CPU1.05345ms. The context did NOT adopt interval0; this is not proof that completely disabling context vsync has no effect. No basis to ship a swap-format change.\n- Actual render submissions are tagged with current camera-request identity and matched to following frameSwapped. This narrows the old arbitrary-mouse-event correlation problem, but does not prove physical-display presentation or native-input UX. No screenshot/readback oracle was added.\n- Source review: Qt redraw calls surface.update directly, camera motion mutates camera immediately, render builds a fresh camera frame, and uniforms retain that frame object rather than a recyclable numeric id. No delayed-camera queue or stale-camera defect found in this route. 2ms polling is allocation-heavy but captured Python cost (~0.65s/45s) does not establish it as the severe-lag cause.\n- Both diagnostic commands exit0. Exact commands/logs/results in C:/Github/ANY3dView/reports/qt-presentation-20261014/swap{0,1}*; harness and probe in same directory. Process wall8.604575000004843s +8.429324699973222s =17.033899699978065s. Inherited GUI cumulative543.5694559999781s; remaining56.43054400002194s. Unit/fake-test budget300s remains untouched. No numerical jobs, ANYfem source edits or Git actions.\n- Review status: source inspected by Astra; no new implementation or independent implementation review. Existing dirty changes untouched. Remaining: reproduce user-visible native-input lag at the reported viewport/runtime and correlate camera identity/actual content; test buttons separately from camera events. Return remaining budget to ANYfem owner; do not label previous software optimization or present timing as a fix for this report.\r\n\n### Sustained capture and bounded lifecycle repair\n\nUser renewed sustained-session scope via ANYfem owner; no additional timed windows were launched here. Capture user-session-1/result.json contains 45 states: GPU valid/visible, no diagnostics or render errors. Stall samples point to native QOpenGLWidget construction and application-side geometry preparation; they do not establish a stale deferred failure as the reported lag/black-canvas cause. Black startup remains unresolved; later white canvas and geometry visibility are not startup acceptance.\n\nConfirmed independent source defect: deferred render failure handled current surface/error without retaining the failed attempt identity. Repair in src/any3dview/qt.py: _GLSurface._defer_render_failure captures source surface and error; _render_failed ignores closed, software, replaced, recovered or newer-error states. Real current failure retains prior reporting/release/fallback behavior. Both initializeGL and paintGL use the new private helper. No public API/renderer architecture or application edits; all pre-existing dirty viewer changes retained. No nested workers; parent reports Mistral availability issues and owns independent integration review.\n\nFocused no-window tests in tests/test_qt_deferred_failure.py: initial baseline6 failed (new signature absent); expanded baseline7 failed including existing no-argument recovered-surface path. Candidate7 passed, then9 passed after actual deferred-callback capture tests. Exact command: C:/Python/Python314/python.exe -m pytest tests/test_qt_deferred_failure.py -q -p no:cacheprovider. Four unique logs/receipts under reports/qt-presentation-20261014/failure-*. No QApplication, native window, GUI benchmark or numerical execution. Baseline logs retained; environment emitted Python-location/pyreadline shutdown warnings, candidate pytest exits0. Product repair is verified by focused fake tests only; native lifecycle behavior and perceived latency not newly accepted. Review status: source self-review, independent integration review outstanding. The existing user-controlled PID24672 was not touched.\r\n\n### Sustained rotation capture 2 (read-only follow-up)\n\nInspected ANYfem user-session-2/result.json (202.5718282s user-controlled session; not a new run by this owner). All163 backend samples report GPU, valid context, visible surface, empty diagnostics/no render error. Right-button surface motion209 events: handler median0.0862ms/p950.1358ms/max3.5181ms; native queue median16ms/p9532ms/max32ms. Source _begin_interaction_scene returns immediately on GPU, excluding its deliberate reduced-scene transition in this state. No evidence of mid-drag software fallback.\n\nThree sampled stalls: initial native widget construction; diagnostic change_style callback; left-click query building ProjectedSelectionIndex (~232.7ms handler,375ms queued). The last is a real selection-path stall, not evidence for sustained right-button orbit lock. Do not treat nested menu/dialog callback duration as continuous main-thread blockage; capture1's2218ms menu duration included confirmation think time and heartbeat activity.\n\nNo new repair is justified from capture2 alone. Missing discriminator: for each right-drag motion, record input delta, _drag mode and camera pose before/after; tag pose actually submitted to render and subsequent swap, then establish GPU completion only with separately authorized targeted measurement if needed. If camera stops changing, inspect focus/cancellation/clamping; if it advances but render does not, inspect redraw/paint delivery; if both advance but display freezes, inspect GPU/presentation. Existing frameSwapped timestamps alone cannot choose among these. No new window, benchmark, test or source edit in this follow-up; lifecycle guard remains separate from reported lag.\r\n\n### Concrete next diagnostic artifact (no new GUI execution)\n\nPrepared tools/trace_qt_camera.py: attach CameraPresentationTrace(viewer) after successful GPU startup, save snapshot() with the existing user-session capture, close() to restore hooks. No windows, timers, automatic closure, file writes or GPU synchronization. Bounded10,000-event memory records inputXY/priorXY, drag mode and camera before/after motion; press/release/cancel; actual renderer submission pose/id; Qt swap linked to last submitted pose. Context/backend replacement is explicitly reported as invalidating the original attachment; reattach instead of silently claiming continuity. This supplies the missing discriminator, not a performance fix or physical-presentation oracle.\n\nInspected dirty renderer diff: frame-stamp camera upload cache predates these dirty edits; each render constructs a fresh _CameraFrame and stamp stores its object, not a recyclable id. Added headless current-source test verifying same-frame reuse, changed orbit view/origin uploads and changed-FOV projection uploads at1932x1069. Four fake tests pass (trace pose/cancellation identity, bounded storage/ownership-safe restoration, failed draw propagation, actual renderer uniform write logic). Command: python -m pytest tests/test_qt_camera_trace.py -q -p no:cacheprovider. Exact command/status/process-wall receipts in trace-candidate and trace-uniform-candidate evidence. Tests inspect CPU-issued uniform bytes, not driver completion. No clean-HEAD substitution or API narrowing; live dirty source retained. Trace has not been attached to user windows by this owner.\r\n\n### Pose correlation and application swap-policy candidate\n\nCapture3 trace:172 distinct motion IDs could be matched to a later Qt swap, zero mismatches between motion-after pose and associated submitted pose; p95 matched input-to-swap16.872895ms. Other motions may be coalesced or lack a following recorded swap; this is not a physical-display/GPU-completion oracle. User video/whole-window drag freeze shifts the principal question to top-level composition/presentation; parent owns video inspection.\n\nQt official docs confirm QOpenGLWidget enables OpenGL composition for the whole top-level window, and inserting the first widget into an already shown hierarchy can recreate the native window (https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qopenglwidget.html). Qt frameSwapped follows top-level composition/swapBuffers, not an independent camera-only display guarantee. Requested swap interval may be overridden/ignored by the platform (https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qsurfaceformat.html#setSwapInterval). Correct prior interpretation: context1/window0 is NOT evidence that the top-level composition request was ineffective; it was a mixed-format request lacking visual acceptance.\n\nConcrete configuration counterexample, no QApplication/window: after setDefaultFormat(format_with_interval0), defaultFormat().swapInterval()==0 but new QSurfaceFormat().swapInterval()==1. Viewer created the latter and explicitly applied it. Compatible candidate: _gl_surface_format() keeps prior OpenGL3.3 core/depth24/MSAA setup and copies only application-default swap interval; default behavior remains1 unless application explicitly requests otherwise. No forced-vsync-off policy, driver changes or application source changes.\n\nNext discriminating user-session configuration (parent owns execution/acceptance): BEFORE QApplication construction, copy QSurfaceFormat.defaultFormat(), setSwapInterval(0), and setDefaultFormat(copy); new viewer now honors that request as well. Record top-level window format AND widget/context formats, GL vendor/renderer and actual user response to orbit/controls/whole-window dragging. Keep same scene/size/runtime, no forced finish or automatic close. App process exit restores the scoped request; do not persist settings or infer physical smoothness from timing alone. No new GUI run by this owner.\n\n12 focused no-window checks pass (3 interval0/1/2 format tests preserving renderer requirements and global format;9 lifecycle tests). Exact command/exit/wallreceipt in surface-format-candidate evidence. Additional process-wall2.083843100001104s; renewed unit allowance remaining99.8604046s. Source/tests implemented locally, uncommitted, self-reviewed; independent integration review outstanding. Actual native activation and user-visible lag fix remain unverified.\r\n\n### Qt persistent update pump candidate\n\nRequested bounded source repair replaces Qt's inherited after(2) allocation loop with one persistent parent-owned QTimer at the SAME2ms interval. Shared ViewerScheduler remains authoritative: FIFO, owner-thread drain,1024-callback limit and callback error propagation. No queue/domain logic duplicated. Constructor invokes _start_update_pump only after successful backend probe; initial drain occurs before timer creation, so callback failure/closure cannot orphan a running pump. Idempotent start never multiplies timers. Qt destroy stops/deletes owned timers and closes the shared scheduler, discarding pending updates and rejecting late submissions. One-shot animation/wheel timers are unchanged; Tk host behavior unchanged.\n\nEvidence: tests/test_qt_update_pump.py uses QCoreApplication/QObject and a real QTimer, no QWidget/GPU/native window. Covers100idle ticks with one timer, repeatedstart, worker FIFO delivered on owner via actual Qt eventloop, bounded1024+1 draining, stale tick/post-close submission, close during drain, wrong-thread refusal, initial callback failure. Existing tests/test_scheduler.py included. Final9passed, exit0; initial6passed retained before adding the startup-failure safeguard. Unique receipts persistent-pump-candidate and persistent-pump-final record2.3498625000356697s and2.29280309996102s process wall. Viewer unit allowance remaining95.2177390s ofrenewed120s after all recorded viewer checks. Python environment shutdown warnings retained inlogs.\n\nOnly proven saving is allocation count: no per-poll QObject churn; wakeup frequency unchanged. No measured native performance/user acceptance, and no claim that this causes/fixes five-second control delay. Existing user pythonw process was not modified/restarted/closed; candidate applies to new module loads. Self-reviewed source and tests, independent integration review outstanding. All unrelated dirty viewer work preserved; noANYfemedit/Gitdelivery/numericalruns.\r\n\n### Software responsiveness candidate — finished handoff\n\nCapture4 timeline supports this narrower route: popup exposure repaints an unchanged software scene for~1.2s, delaying the next control event; obsolete hover movements can synchronously build the CPU selection index. Implemented in existing owned source:\n\n1. _PaintSurface caches the completed full-quality QImage. Exposure/popups repaint that image without rerunning geometry work. Invalidation: explicit redraw revision (scene/appearance/selection mutations), camera position/target/up/FOV/near/far, logicalsize/DPR and background. No decimation or image-quality reduction; physical raster and device-ratio transform retained.\n2. Software-only hover coalesces until120ms pointer quiet using one owned timer. Leave/hide/focusout/press/wheel/drag cancel pending work; delivery checks closed/current surface/underMouse/drag state. GPU hover route unchanged. Click/box selection dispatch unchanged and exact.\n3. _cancel_interaction previously requested a full redraw even on idle focus transfer. It now resets all interaction state without redraw when neither camera drag nor selection overlay was active. Real drag/overlay cancellation still redraws. This avoids defeating the new cache on firstcontrol focus.\n4. Previously delivered persistent2ms pump, stalefailure guard and app swap-policy support remain separate deltas, not evidence of this responsiveness fix.\n\nVerification: final24passed across tests/test_qt_software_responsiveness.py, tests/test_qt_update_pump.py and tests/test_scheduler.py. Includes pixel-exact cached/direct QImage painting at DPR1/1.5/2, actual software scene renderer comparison through highlight/section/visibility mutations, reuse without extra renderer frames, camera/redraw invalidation, obsoletehover cancellation, unchanged exactclick dispatch, idle versus active cancellation, FIFO/close/error pump checks. No native QWidget/window/GPU probe. Actual-scene oracle intentionally omits font HUD to stay QImage-only; native assembled Qt/HUD/persistence checks are parent's responsibility.\n\nPreserved failures: software-response-scene lacked fakehost.make_current; software-response-scene-fixed patched QPainter as callable without Antialiasing. Fixed harness, retained assertions, final actualscene oracle passes. All stdout/stderr and exact command/process-wall/exit receipts remain under reports/qt-presentation-20261014; final evidence software-response-focus-final.*. No claim from failing partial runs. Current cumulative renewed viewer-unit spend40.332294600026216s; remaining79.66770539997378s of120s (all attempts counted).\n\nLimits: necessary full-quality rerender after actual scene/camera change remains synchronous; settled hover can still take time once begun. This candidate removes redundant exposure/focus redraws and cancelled-transit hover, not all software rendering latency. No liveuser acceptance or broader GPU-lock fix claimed. No newGUI/process launch, originalwindows untouched, no commits/push/ANYfemsource edits. Self-review done; parent independent assembled validation pending. Candidate final source identities are saved in software-response-source-manifest.json.\r\n\n### Post-Windows-reboot prevention/evidence slice\n\nUser clarified recovery followed a FULL WINDOWS REBOOT, not app restart. Historical/current source comparisons did not isolate root cause; prior whole-system freeze and subsequent health leave driver/DWM/Qt/shared GPU state and application lifecycle as hypotheses. No native rendering campaign is authorized in this slice.\n\nDelegated coherent Qt lifecycle implementation to Mistral via configured wrapper, max28turns, earlycheckpoint byturn6, finalverification/handoff reserve. Ownership qt.py +optionalprivatehelper +new lifecycle tests/evidence; no renderer/HUD sibling edits without concrete boundary decision, noGit/GUI/numericals. Worker check budget30s precise process wall fromremaining79.6677054s, ownerreserve49.6677054s. Existing caches/pump/hover/swap/failureguard and otherdirty edits protected.\n\nSource findings guiding acceptance: HUD-construction failure bypasses HUD-gated renderer cleanup; one resource-release exception skips subsequent cleanup and doneCurrent; destroy repeats renderer.release; aboutToBeDestroyed callback lacks context-generation binding; grabFramebuffer exception bypasses explicitconstructor destroy. Repair must establish resource ownership, idempotent teardown, partial-failure handling and stale-context isolation without issuing GL work on a wrong/dead context. Native context remains Qt-owned. Passive optionaldiagnostic_snapshot must use cached GL identity captured only duringinitializeGL and Qt validity, return independent JSON-compatible data, and never render/makeCurrent/liveGLquery/enumerateprocesses/accessproject. Fake tests can support these local contracts, not demonstrate reboot-cause or universal prevention.\r\n\n### Lifecycle slice final handoff (host ownership and passive diagnosis)\n\nMistral delivered the central host lifecycle implementation across three bounded continuations, with useful source/tests but repeatedly ended mid-verification without final checkpoint. Astra inspected each partial artifact and supplied precise deltas. After repeated incomplete handoffs and the newly reported production deleted-surface failure, Astra completed the tightly coupled context-ordering/teardown integration and verification directly; preserved all worker/failure evidence. No fresh worker budget or GUI authority was inferred.\n\nANYfem independently found the production result-switch caller defect: retained-result handles were reused across GPUviewer replacement, then their old viewer was destroyed. Caller cache-owner repair stays inANYfem. Viewer protection complements it: make_current refuses a CLOSED viewer before touching native surface; cleanup/fallback tolerate already-deleted wrappers. No numerical solve/model/resultvalidation changes.\n\nImplemented host guarantees:\n- Explicit Qt-context ownership and ready state, cached GLstrings while initializeGL current; same-context successful initialization is reused, incomplete replay initialization is cleaned/rebuilt, replaced contexts cannot receive oldresources' release calls or be spuriously unbound.\n- Exact aboutToBeDestroyed slot captured/disconnected; stale signal and failure callbacks cannot release replacements. Surface identity checked BEFOREmakeCurrent, actual QtcurrentContext checked BEFORE GLrelease. Invalid/deleted contexts skip unsafe GLcalls and retain diagnostics.\n- HUD-construction failure releases the constructed renderer; cleanup errors preserve the primary initialization error. Raw grabFramebuffer failure rolls back. HUD/renderer teardown attempted independently and doneCurrent paired. Owner releases GLresources once. Closed/deleted timer/widget exceptions do not prevent scheduler/timer/resource shutdown or remaining hide/delete attempts.\n- Optional diagnostic_snapshot(): schema='any3dview.qt.lifecycle',version=1,active_backend,closed,context_valid,context_ready,gl_vendor/gl_renderer/gl_version (cached lastknown), lifecycle_counters andboundedlifecycle_errors. Independent JSONcopies; only QtisValid andcachedstate read, no liveGLinfo/makeCurrent/render/backend switch/process/projectaccess. NomandatoryViewerBackendchange.\n\nFinal checks: exact command and full evidence in lifecycle-worker/lifecycle-unit2-root-final-receipt.json: tests/test_qt_resource_lifecycle.py tests/test_qt_deferred_failure.py tests/test_qt_update_pump.py tests/test_qt_diagnostic_snapshot_oracle.py -p no:cacheprovider -q =>58passed,exit0. Root independent poison-resource snapshot oracle included. NoQApplication/nativewindow/GPU/numericalrun. gitdiff--check clean forownedsource. Earlier failures retained: two draftfake/expectation issues, missinghelper inPump regression, unhashablefake timer; all corresponding finalcases pass. Thirdowner continuation changed source without remainingtesthandoff; root completedfinaltests rather than claiming its verification.\n\nUnit accounting for this slice: oldrounded receipts2.154+1.955+2.051 conservativelycharged6.1615s including.0005perrun uncertainty; newraw1.8778075000000172s +2.374799699999812s. Totalupper10.4141072s fromremaining79.6677054s => remainingatleast69.2535982s. No rerun to reconstructoldprecision. Full native lifecycle/useracceptance and rootindependentintegrationreview outstanding.\n\nKnown boundary NOT claimed fixed: internal partial allocations inside ModernGLRenderer/GPUHudRenderer constructors before they return, and per-resource failure continuation inside their own release() implementations, are outside this host-only repair. Host catches/reports release failures but cannot guarantee their internal resources all freed. Do NOT apply the existing classmethod resource-stage decorator naively to __init__: it expectsreturnedobject.ctx and omitsprogram allocations. Further allocator/renderer work needs a bounded dedicated follow-through if adopted. No reboot rootcause/preventionclaim, no commits/pushes. Final sourceidentity stored lifecycle-source-manifest.json; source now ready forANYfem integrationreview.\r\n\n### Performance review — 2026-10-19\n\nScope: current dirty ANY3dView source at HEAD cba89a7, with read-only ANYfem caller inspection. Review authorization only; no product edits, native/GPU/solver runs or renewed historical benchmark budget. Adopted ecosystem policy unchanged. Question: which costs remain after prior barge fixes, and which evidence distinguishes CPU work from presentation stalls? Source inspection plus preserved controlled measurements support prioritization, not new speedup claims.\n\nFindings:\n1. GPU hover: qt.py mouseMoveEvent immediately calls _hover_select for each unpressed GPU move; renderer.pick_detail synchronously reads the ID framebuffer even when the ID image is cached. Coalesce optional hover to one latest request per frame; preserve exact clicks and stale-camera cancellation. GPU/driver stall impact remains unmeasured.\n2. Software motion: _paint_software still transforms/projects all vertices, allocates Python per-primitive objects, sorts surfaces and submits individual QPainter polygons per changed camera. Completed-image caching helps exposure only. Prior frozen 5,280-triangle fixture measured 272.38ms median; 20,000-triangle fixture 1066.89ms. These are historical six-tick measurements, not current performance guarantees. Prioritize retained topology/world-space/scalar-color work and vectorized admission; any reduced-detail interaction is a separate explicit quality decision.\n3. Retained animation: _show_animation_frame removes old meshes and constructs/adds new handles every frame. Qt calls this route and ANYfem tasks.py invokes play_animation. This prevents GPU geometry reuse even for topology-identical frames. Retain geometry across compatible frames and update only deformation/scalars, preserving ownership/cancellation/restoration. Savings unmeasured.\n4. CPU selection: _selection_key includes the complete generations value, including scalar/appearance, so scalar-only changes invalidate projected geometry. Narrow dependencies while retaining visibility, topology, deformation, camera, section and pick-owner correctness. GPU sync already excludes scalar/appearance from its analogous ID dirty condition.\n5. Idle scheduling: Qt persistent update timer polls every2ms even when idle (~500 scheduled opportunities/second/viewer, actual wakeups not measured). Prefer owner-thread queued wakeup with bounded drain and no-lost-wakeup/FIFO/close tests; lower priority than interactive stalls.\n\nPositive existing design: retained GPU geometry, generation-based scalar updates, cached ID target, frame-local projection, exposure raster cache and bounded hover debounce on software. Do not replace these with full-scene rebuilds. Historical request-to-swap ~16.9ms p95 does not prove physical-display latency; Windows reboot clearing lag remains unexplained. Most viewer changes are still dirty/unpublished; source and installed application must be identified separately before acceptance.\n\nSuggested next measurement contract (not executed): freeze exact example/mesh/runtime/display settings; measure sustained orbit/pan/zoom and hover, result animation, field switches, idle wakeups and lifecycle cycles; record p50/p95/p99 input-to-present, handler and render durations, upload counts and memory trend. Proposed target:60Hz navigation (16.7ms frame budget), bounded control latency and no steady memory growth; user hardware and visual quality determine final acceptance. Compare changed paths only, preserve image/picking/scalar correctness.\n\nIndependent Mistral Read attempt failed before tool inspection because .vibe/logs/vibe.log was inaccessible. No worker findings or independent-review claim. Astra completed source review; no test pass or measured savings claimed this turn.\r\n\n### Five-improvement implementation plan — 2026-10-19\nUser authorizes all five repairs and fresh bounded barge performance verification, preserving visual quality and exact picking. This is new execution authority for this workload, not reuse of old consumed budgets. Preserve current dirty source; frozen baseline at reports/performance-20261019/baseline-src and preexisting.patch. No solver/mesher jobs, package publication or unrelated edits.\nSlices: (1) GPU hover latest-event coalescing, exact clicks unchanged; (2) retained software preparation/cache avoiding per-camera invariant work, identical pixels; (3) compatible animation handle reuse, correctness across incompatible frames/cancel/restore; (4) CPU selection geometry-only generation invalidation; (5) queued scheduler notifications with owner-thread bounded FIFO and closure. One implementation owner spans coupled Qt/retained files. Acceptance: regression tests for invalidation and refusal, barge before/after timings on frozen same source fixture and poses, pixel and picking parity; counts distinguish caching from skipped work. Native runs serial, each <=120s, initial aggregate benchmark cap600s; focused tests each <=120s. Timings and failures retained with exact source/environment. No reduced visual detail or approximate selection. Review and benchmark integration owned by Astra.\r\n", "numLines": 163, "startLine": 1, "requestedOffset": null, "requestedLimit": 2000, "totalLines": null, "wasTruncated": false}, "outputText": "", "durationMs": 0.0, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Read", "message": "163 lines from C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\docs\\BARGE_VIEW_PERFORMANCE.md", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": ""}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
