{"id": "effect-83cc4279-7225-501a-a82c-590904f0c24d", "sessionId": "0c772a71-d421-e6a8-14d9-dcb034248b0b", "turnId": "turn-2f430b3805d99f342897b43671b5e559", "createdAt": 1792406790240, "updatedAt": 1792406790302, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "effect", "title": "file_system.read_file", "detail": {"toolName": "file_system.read_file", "display": {"summary": "Reading C:/Github/ANYopenSoft/governance/ECOSYSTEM_PHILOSOPHY.md", "content": null, "suffix": "", "verb": "Reading", "message": "C:/Github/ANYopenSoft/governance/ECOSYSTEM_PHILOSOPHY.md", "settledVerb": "Read", "settledMessage": "C:/Github/ANYopenSoft/governance/ECOSYSTEM_PHILOSOPHY.md", "statusText": "Reading file"}, "kind": "file_read", "input": {"filePath": "C:/Github/ANYopenSoft/governance/ECOSYSTEM_PHILOSOPHY.md", "offset": null, "limit": 2000}}, "state": {"status": "completed", "output": {"filePath": "C:\\Github\\ANYopenSoft\\governance\\ECOSYSTEM_PHILOSOPHY.md", "content": "# ANY Ecosystem Philosophy\r\n\r\n## Canonical ownership\r\n\r\nEach domain has one authoritative owner and dependencies flow downstream:\r\n\r\n- ANYgeometry owns geometry, topology, persistent identity, tolerance,\r\n  spatial indexing, intersections, and geometry audit truth.\r\n- ANYmesh owns discretization and consumes qualified geometry contracts.\r\n- ANYmaterial owns material definitions, validation, and canonical conventions.\r\n- ANYio/ANYfileio owns interchange syntax and neutral file semantics.\r\n- ANYsolver owns solver physics, numerical kernels, and qualification scope.\r\n- ANYfem owns FEM workflow, projects, orchestration, and postprocessing.\r\n- UI/application repositories consume public APIs and do not recreate domain truth.\r\n\r\nCopied owner models, reverse imports, and coordinate-derived replacements for\r\nowner topology are architectural defects. Compatibility facades may exist only\r\nas thin, explicit, tested aliases.\r\n\r\n## Contract doctrine\r\n\r\n1. Prefer headless, public, stable contracts. Integration and UI layers use\r\n   public APIs rather than private helpers.\r\n2. Fail closed. Unsupported, unqualified, stale, wrong-model, or ambiguous\r\n   inputs produce typed non-success; downstream preserves that truth.\r\n3. Do not invent, narrow, or silently discard semantics. Preserve unknown\r\n   interchange records, absent components, provenance, material symmetry, and\r\n   solver-reported status.\r\n4. Identity is model-bound and persistent. IDs are not guessed from coordinates,\r\n   silently reused, or detached from revision/lineage.\r\n5. Public mutation is atomic and rollback-safe. Incremental work follows explicit\r\n   change sets and invalidation, not tolerance-based global rediscovery.\r\n6. Boundary conventions are ecosystem contracts: SI internally, conversion once\r\n   at ingestion/display, canonical material Voigt order, and structural protocols\r\n   rather than concrete cross-package inheritance.\r\n\r\n## Evidence and qualification\r\n\r\n- Source and tests are behavioral truth. Reports are commit-, command-, and\r\n  environment-scoped evidence, not timeless capability declarations.\r\n- Qualification is narrower than implementation. A downstream application cannot\r\n  broaden the solver owner's qualified scope.\r\n- Partial runs, skipped execution, XFAIL, deck generation, and `not_executed` are\r\n  never reported as passes.\r\n- Failure evidence is retained. Plausible estimates, zeros, or fallbacks never\r\n  replace a real non-success result.\r\n- Every capability claim names the exact implementation identity, source SHA,\r\n  contract version, command, result, platform, and remaining limits.\r\n\r\n## Performance doctrine\r\n\r\nCorrectness and sound theory rank above performance. Implementations must be\r\nphysics-first where physics is involved and independently verifiable. Artificial\r\nor hidden penalties, empirical tuning, invented stiffness, and other “magic”\r\ncorrections may not be introduced merely to satisfy a numerical or performance\r\ngate. A derived formulation requires an explicit identity, derivation,\r\nprovenance, and qualification.\r\n\r\nPerformance cannot change semantics. Every acceleration keeps a correctness\r\noracle, explicit eligibility, lifecycle, invalidation, deterministic diagnostics,\r\nand fail-closed fallback rules. Timing alone does not prove activation or\r\ncorrectness.\r\n\r\nNo production stage may retain demonstrated global-quadratic behavior where the\r\ngoverning plan requires subquadratic scaling. Memory evidence distinguishes\r\nPython-traced allocation from process RSS and native allocation. Performance\r\nqualification follows the effective workspace resource policy and any task-specific\r\nresource limits. In Normal-token mode, worker-owned scheduling applies under\r\n`C:/Github/AGENTS.md`; historical lease rules do not reactivate themselves.\r\n\r\n## Long-term stance\r\n\r\nPrefer additive migration paths, explicit versions, and reversible compatibility.\r\nRetire duplicated ownership rather than institutionalizing it. Frozen historical\r\ntrees remain historical and never regain authority through convenience imports.\r\n\r\n## Adopted engineering and delivery policy\r\n\r\nThe following revision governs future development procedure across the ecosystem.\r\nIt supplements the ownership and technical doctrine above. Existing scientific,\r\nrelease and task-specific execution boundaries remain in force as specified below.\r\n\r\n### Risk-Proportionate Engineering and Delivery\r\n\r\n**Policy ID:** `ANY_ECOSYSTEM_RISK_PROPORTIONATE_ENGINEERING_V1`\r\n\r\n**Date:** 2026-09-24\r\n\r\n**Status:** Adopted by user direction, effective 2026-09-24. Revision: `2026-09-24.2`. Canonical home: `ANYopenSoft/governance/ECOSYSTEM_PHILOSOPHY.md`.\r\n\r\n**Scope:** All ANY packages, applications, research tools, integration workflows and participating human/AI development agents, including ECOboss coordination where used.\r\n\r\n> **Develop iteratively, verify throughout, qualify the intended use, and preserve the evidence supporting the claim. Apply rigor to consequential failure modes; do not turn every development experiment into a qualification ceremony.**\r\n\r\n### 1. Purpose and unchanged principles\r\n\r\nDeliver useful, trustworthy engineering workflows. Measure progress by a working capability, a resolved uncertainty or a demonstrated improvement—not by the number of plans, tests, agents, review rounds or archived files.\r\n\r\nScientific correctness, numerical honesty, user-data integrity and safe execution remain non-negotiable. Performance, lightweight deployment, maintainability, accessible interfaces and reproducibility remain primary design objectives. Optimize measured end-to-end work without silently changing the model, accuracy, admissible domain or reported meaning of results.\r\n\r\nPreserve package ownership and interfaces. Reuse existing numerical kernels, transaction stores, geometry/topology services, material definitions, file formats and command layers before introducing competing infrastructure. Keep the open engineering packages independently useful; this update does not change any license or the boundary of proprietary products.\r\n\r\nScope assurance by **consequence, technical uncertainty, exposure and intended claim**, not by filename or project size. A research oracle, result-unit conversion or GUI command can be as consequential as a numerical kernel. Conversely, an isolated presentation change does not require a mechanics qualification programme.\r\n\r\nThis policy changes how development is organized. It does not itself qualify software, authorize a release, broaden an existing capability, waive a scientific requirement or override an explicit user restriction.\r\n\r\n### 2. Assess risk and maturity separately\r\n\r\nAt the start of a substantial task, record the intended outcome, the consequential failure modes and the required acceptance evidence in the existing task note or pull request. A few sentences normally suffice; no new risk-scoring application or mandatory numerical risk score is required.\r\n\r\n| Change risk | Typical examples | Proportionate assurance |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| Low, local and readily reversible | Editorial wording or layout with no change to numerical meaning, permissions or stored data | Normal review/CI and focused checks. No scientific acceptance dossier. |\r\n| Behavioral or cross-package | New adapters, workflows, APIs, packaging or performance paths | Focused regression and end-to-end tests, compatibility review and honest capability documentation. Escalate where errors can silently change engineering results. |\r\n| High consequence or difficult to detect | Mechanics, constitutive histories, units/frames, topology identity, constraints, numerical reference tools, uncertainty/fatigue calculations, restart, contact history, destructive migrations or privileged AI commands | Early risk-focused technical review, independent verification appropriate to the claim, and controlled acceptance before the changed behavior is relied upon. |\r\n\r\nThese examples are not automatic classifications. Reassess when exposure, dependencies, supported combinations or the proposed claim change. Explain a downgrade; do not relabel high-risk work merely to avoid a gate. Formal external obligations remain applicable.\r\n\r\nMaturity is a separate dimension:\r\n\r\n| Level | Purpose and permitted use | Minimum record and review |\r\n|---|---|---|\r\n| **A — Development and investigation** | Explore, implement, refactor, diagnose and run authorized integrated experiments. Results are provisional and cannot confer qualification. | Recoverable source, relevant tests, commands/inputs needed to reproduce important findings, and normal change review. Add early independent technical scrutiny for high-risk assumptions; do not require a formal acceptance package for every helper. |\r\n| **B — Candidate acceptance** | Establish a named scientific, numerical, interoperability or performance claim within a declared domain. | Prospective criteria, exact candidate identity, controlled relevant runs, retained raw evidence and an independent acceptance assessment. Include a pre-run review when a governing contract or the risk requires it. |\r\n| **C — Supported delivery or release** | Make the admitted capability available through a supported interface, artifact, default or release. | Impact-appropriate regression/qualification, matching installed-artifact checks, compatibility, recovery/restart/resource checks where applicable, release notes and the existing approval process. Reuse valid B evidence rather than regenerate it automatically. |\r\n\r\nA routine low-risk patch does not need an artificial Level-B scientific study before release. High-risk development does not become low-risk because it lives under `scripts/`, is opt-in or is labelled experimental. Packaging, merge status, public exposure and scientific qualification remain distinct decisions.\r\n\r\n### 3. Default development loop\r\n\r\nUse the shortest loop that can establish the next meaningful result:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nDefine the outcome and important failure modes\r\n    -> inspect/reuse existing implementation and evidence\r\n    -> implement a bounded end-to-end slice\r\n    -> run local and integration checks\r\n    -> investigate discrepancies with targeted independent checks\r\n    -> retain the useful result or reject the approach\r\n    -> repeat until a claim-bearing candidate is ready\r\n```\r\n\r\nUnit tests and small algebraic checks support this loop; they are not substitutes for exercising the assembled workflow. Run realistic integrated cases early enough to expose interface and modelling problems, inside the authorized execution scope and resource budget.\r\n\r\nDo not automatically create a separate preregistration, immutable proof root, one-use adjudicator, source-binding framework or formal review milestone for each helper, bug fix or experiment. Use existing CI, test utilities and task records. A helper needs a separate authority package only when a concrete risk, reuse role or governing contract justifies it.\r\n\r\nUse development commits and reproducible run records, not a mandatory fixed commit count. New experiments may be rerun and redesigned unless a specific restriction applies. Record material changes and failures; do not repeatedly rerun a nondeterministic test until it happens to pass.\r\n\r\nA blocked exploratory attempt should normally produce a diagnosis and a bounded next action. Reserve fail-closed acceptance behavior for the trust boundary: invalid or unsupported results must not be used or presented as qualified. Do not disable runtime safety/capability guards to speed development; isolate a deliberately diagnostic route when necessary.\r\n\r\n#### Decision-oriented investigation\r\n\r\nOrganize exploratory work around the decision it must enable. Before a\r\nsubstantial diagnostic batch, briefly identify the unresolved question,\r\nthe competing explanations, the bounded experiment that distinguishes\r\nthem, and how its possible outcomes affect the next action.\r\n\r\nRepeated refinement, parameter sweeps or additional reference tooling\r\nrequire a stated reason tied to the principal uncertainty. At the end\r\nof a batch, interpret the combined evidence and decide whether to\r\ncontinue, change the approach, advance an independent workstream, or\r\nreport a specific unresolved limitation. Do not automatically repeat\r\nthe same experiment at greater cost.\r\n\r\nUse the existing living task record; this is not a new registration,\r\napproval or reporting gate. Developers retain discretion within their\r\nauthority and resource limits. Diagnostic stopping rules do not replace\r\nscientific acceptance criteria, and exhausting a diagnostic budget does\r\nnot prove that a formulation has failed.\r\n\r\n### 4. Review where it can change the decision\r\n\r\nNormal engineering review remains part of development. Independent review is not forbidden at Level A and must not be postponed until release when a high-risk assumption will shape the implementation or the next experiment.\r\n\r\nPrefer one review owner coordinating the relevant expertise and reusing resolved findings. Add specialist review for concrete mechanics, numerics, security or interoperability risks. Do not require a fixed agent count, multiple model votes or repeated complete rereviews after unrelated edits. Review changed and affected areas; widen the review when their dependencies justify it.\r\n\r\nA useful independent check can be an analytical result, separately derived formulation, manufactured-work calculation, different integration/solution route, derivative oracle or compatible external reference. Match it to the claim. Two wrappers around the same implementation are not independent verification. Two AI agents or differently named scripts do not by themselves establish scientific independence or human approval.\r\n\r\nReviewers should distinguish a correctness blocker from a documentation clarification and an optional improvement. An additional blocking control must identify the failure mode it addresses and the decision it affects. This is not a restriction on discovering defects; it prevents unrelated framework expansion from becoming a completion condition.\r\n\r\n### 5. Candidate acceptance: define criteria before the assessment run\r\n\r\nThe earlier shorthand “freeze claims, not experiments” is replaced by this more precise rule:\r\n\r\n> **Explore freely within authority. Define and freeze the acceptance question, inputs, metrics and criteria before the formal assessment. Preserve the complete evidence when accepting or rejecting the claim.**\r\n\r\nFor a scientific/numerical candidate, the concise acceptance plan must identify the physical or mathematical problem, admitted domain, source/data identity, solver options, relevant integration and scaling, observables, reference/oracle, tolerances, resource limits, failure handling and result interpretation. Include conditioning, mesh/time refinement, state history or work/energy checks where they are material—not as a universal checklist for every change.\r\n\r\nKnown development cases may remain regression benchmarks. Disclose that they informed development; do not call them blind validation. Do not retrofit acceptance limits to observed candidate values. A justified change to an acceptance criterion requires an explicit new decision and prospective assessment; the previous failed result remains failed under its original criterion.\r\n\r\nA candidate package ordinarily needs one source/environment record, one relevant run collection, and one acceptance report. Source-control identity plus hashes for built or externally stored artifacts can establish provenance without copying and hashing every helper repeatedly. Reuse stricter existing machinery when a current contract requires it.\r\n\r\nRetain actual invoked commands, exit status, relevant inputs, seeds, environment/thread/backend settings and raw quantities sufficient to recompute the verdict. Record the built/installed artifact when installation is part of the claim. Do not report a generated deck as an executed comparison, a small residual as physical accuracy, or a successful reference calculation as qualification of a different product.\r\n\r\nAn independent assessment must address correctness, numerical adequacy, contrary evidence and the scope of the conclusion—not merely file hashes. If a formal run fails, preserve it, return to development, and assess a corrected candidate under a clearly identified revision. Do not restart the entire historical qualification programme unless the affected dependencies require it.\r\n\r\n### 6. Minimum evidence without indiscriminate archiving\r\n\r\nGit preserves committed content, not every untracked log, external result or active worktree. Important evidence therefore needs an explicit home. [R3]\r\n\r\nFor development, retain the minimal reproducible counterexample or fixture, the affected source identity, the observed failure and the fix/regression test. Keep raw data when necessary to understand or reproduce a consequential finding. An exhaustive permanent archive of every terminal message and successful intermediate run is not required.\r\n\r\nDo not selectively discard negative results that challenge a later claim. Repetitive transient logs may follow the ordinary retention policy once meaningful failures, decisions and required inputs are retained. This policy is not authorization to delete historical archives, user files or another worker's outputs.\r\n\r\nFor accepted claims, preserve exact artifacts and enough evidence to reproduce the decision. Use one maintainable manifest where appropriate, rather than recursive inventories of inventories. Bitwise reproducibility is required when it is part of the declared contract or necessary for identity; otherwise specify the appropriate numerical reproducibility across supported environments. Existing exact-equality obligations cannot be relaxed implicitly.\r\n\r\nA checksum proves identity, not physical correctness. A test count proves neither coverage nor an admitted engineering domain. A previous proof applies only to its actual assumptions, implementation and data.\r\n\r\n### 7. Scientific and safety boundaries that remain unchanged\r\n\r\nNever silently alter equations, physical geometry, reference frames, units, load/support definitions, constraints, constitutive state laws, prescribed inertia, reference data, observables, acceptance tolerances or error normalizations to achieve a pass. Numerical changes are permitted when deliberately implemented and assessed as changes—not disguised as administrative simplification.\r\n\r\nPreserve committed/trial ownership, rollback, restart compatibility, capability checks, conservation/work identities where applicable and explicit rejection of unsupported combinations. A faster execution path must retain its stated numerical contract or receive a separately assessed scope.\r\n\r\nReference code and adjudication code require assurance commensurate with the product conclusions that depend on them. Calling a reference implementation “research tooling” does not reduce its consequence. Exact arithmetic and certified error bounds remain necessary where the registered question requires them; they are not blanket prerequisites for unrelated exploration.\r\n\r\nKeep source-reading, test execution, benchmark performance, mathematical verification, physical validation and product release claims separate. Report uncertainty, unresolved numerical admission and nonconvergence honestly. A failed upper-bound certificate is not automatically a demonstrated mechanics defect.\r\n\r\nThis update does not relax licensing, privacy, credential handling, file permissions, network restrictions, paid-resource approvals or destructive-operation controls.\r\n\r\n### 8. Reuse evidence through impact analysis\r\n\r\nBefore rerunning a large campaign, identify the changed behavior and its dependents. Reuse accepted evidence only when the relevant implementation, inputs, assumptions and numerical environment remain applicable. Record why the evidence still applies; do not relabel an old run as execution of a new artifact.\r\n\r\nA documentation-only change normally needs documentation checks, not a new mechanics campaign. A change to a shared return map, frame convention, state owner, parser-unit map or constraint reduction may require broad downstream revalidation. A default/selector change requires explicit scope and compatibility review even if kernel equations are unchanged.\r\n\r\nRespect current release requirements. A partial development suite cannot replace a required full release suite. Any change to such a requirement must be explicitly adopted before it is used, not excused after a failure. The current ANYsolver quality guide already separates development diagnosis from full release evidence. [R4]\r\n\r\n### 9. Performance, resources and practical completion\r\n\r\nProfile complete workflows and cost per accepted result, including failed steps, repeated evaluations, setup, state copies, recovery and serialization. Keep measurement overhead bounded. Prefer simpler proven optimizations to speculative rewrites or new mandatory dependencies.\r\n\r\nTests and experiments must fit declared CPU/GPU, memory, disk, thread and execution permissions. Account for child processes and large factorization/output workspaces when relevant. Development reruns do not imply unlimited compute authority. A resource limit may stop a run, but does not establish physical failure or a successful scientific criterion.\r\n\r\nMaintain one short living task record: outcome, actual state, next integrated result, decisive checks, material findings and remaining blocker. Extend an existing record instead of creating one file per item. If checkpoints keep producing only helper infrastructure without reducing the principal uncertainty, simplify the next experiment or escalate that specific obstruction. Do not invent a new framework as the default response.\r\n\r\nReuse existing publication, CI and packaging infrastructure. Do not make a universal certificate engine, general benchmark platform or new governance service a prerequisite for a bounded engineering result unless the actual task requires it.\r\n\r\n### 10. Agent execution and authority\r\n\r\nThe integration owner coordinates shared files, compatibility and final disposition. Use bounded specialist assignments and isolated worktrees when concurrent work requires them. Group independent reads/tests when efficient. Avoid repeated polling without a decision need. Model names, effort settings and spending follow the user's actual choice; do not silently escalate or claim unobservable settings.\r\n\r\nECOboss or another coordinator applies this policy only within delegated authority. It cannot manufacture approval, silently change an active task's contract, waive scientific criteria, or grant itself broader filesystem/network/release permissions.\r\n\r\nPreserve protected branches, unrelated changes, in-flight runs, committed evidence and worktree ownership. Do not reset, clean, rebase, merge, publish or terminate another worker merely to obtain a convenient baseline. Check actual state before acting; a cached remote reference is not a fresh remote verification.\r\n\r\nA numerical failure, unsupported case or authorization conflict should stop the affected action, not automatically stop every independent part of the task. Continue permitted useful work while clearly identifying the blocked boundary.\r\n\r\n### 11. Adopting the policy and migrating active tasks\r\n\r\n**Implementation order: ecosystem policy first, task steering second.**\r\n\r\n1. Locate and read the actual canonical ecosystem philosophy and the instructions used by its orchestrator and repositories. Merge this policy into that authority; do not create a competing philosophy by guessing a path. If no canonical location exists, the owner selects one and records it.\r\n2. Retain the policy ID above as a stable searchable anchor. Record the adopted file path and commit/revision. Set its effective status through the existing approval process. Update relevant instruction pointers or explicitly distribute the adopted document to workers that cannot access it. Prefer references to one authority over divergent copies.\r\n3. Preserve unrelated philosophy, licenses, technical requirements and release rules. Remove or amend blanket *future administrative* requirements only where this policy explicitly replaces them. Do not rewrite completed contracts or archives.\r\n4. For each active task, add one short prospective transition note to its existing plan: adopted policy reference; administrative steps consolidated; substantive gates retained; next permitted integrated action; and any remaining approval required. Obtain task-owner authorization where its current contract requires amendment. Do not create another full qualification package for this transition.\r\n5. Give the worker an explicit steering instruction to read the adopted policy and the transition note. Do not assume a running agent automatically reloads changed instruction files. Verify that the worker names the same policy revision and retained acceptance boundaries before it changes its process.\r\n\r\n**Conflict rule:** this general policy does not silently supersede a frozen case registration, consumed one-use execution, resource approval, explicit no-run instruction or protected evidence requirement. Amend future procedural requirements explicitly. Keep scientific criteria and historical dispositions intact unless separately changed by the proper authority. When blocked, identify the exact conflict and the smallest required decision; do not ask for approval after every ordinary development action.\r\n\r\n### 12. Definition of completion\r\n\r\nA task is complete when the requested end-to-end outcome works within its declared domain, relevant correctness and compatibility checks pass, risk-appropriate review is closed, results are reproducible at the required level, and any remaining limitations are accurately exposed. Publication and default changes need their own existing authorization.\r\n\r\nA qualification claim requires its specified acceptance evidence. A development task may also close with a supported negative conclusion or a precisely documented unresolved limitation. Neither documentation volume nor a test count substitutes for the requested result.\r\n\r\nSuggested entry in the existing task record:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOutcome and intended use:\r\nRisk / acceptance level, with reason:\r\nCurrent implementation and evidence:\r\nNext integrated test or result:\r\nUnchanged criteria / authority / resource limits:\r\nMaterial findings and affected dependencies:\r\nDecision and remaining limitation:\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Rationale and source basis (not additional compliance obligations)\r\n\r\nThe policy is an ANY-specific recommendation, not a declaration of compliance with NASA standards or any external certification scheme. The earlier suggestion to omit independent review throughout development was too broad. Technical scrutiny remains necessary when consequences warrant it; what is reduced is repeated formal ceremony around intermediate work.\r\n\r\n- **[R1] NASA Software Engineering Handbook, SWE-020 — Software Classification.** Supports tailoring rigor to criticality and intended use, rather than imposing the highest assurance level on every component. It does not authorize ANY task waivers. https://swehb.nasa.gov/spaces/SWEHBVD/pages/102695405/SWE-020%2B-%2BSoftware%2BClassification\r\n- **[R2] NASA Systems Engineering Handbook, Section 2.4 — Verification and Validation.** Supports verification/validation during development and phase-appropriate methods, not deferring all validation to delivery. https://www.nasa.gov/reference/2-4-distinctions-between-product-verification-and-product-validation/\r\n- **[R3] Git `git-status` documentation.** Distinguishes committed/index/working-tree and untracked content; source control is not automatic retention of all experimental data. https://git-scm.com/docs/git-status\r\n- **[R4] ANYsolver, `docs/QUALITY_CONTROL.md`, read 2026-09-24, blob `d126475e3b5a4ae5bf75571f56b31bc0efad391a`.** Already distinguishes partial development checks, full release evidence, executed external comparisons and scoped reports. https://github.com/audunarn/ANYsolver/blob/main/docs/QUALITY_CONTROL.md\r\n- **[R5] NASA-STD-7009B public standard record.** Provides a relevant intended-use/credibility framework for models and simulations; not a substitute for domain-specific engineering validation. https://standards.nasa.gov/node/263\r\n\r\nThe F03 status reports supplied by the user demonstrate that independent checks found consequential representation, constraint, integration and sparse-storage defects. They support keeping those technical protections. They do not establish how much wall-clock effort can safely be removed, nor demonstrate physical convergence. Expected productivity benefits of this policy must be assessed from actual delivery, defect and rework outcomes.\r\n", "numLines": 289, "startLine": 1, "requestedOffset": null, "requestedLimit": 2000, "totalLines": null, "wasTruncated": false}, "outputText": "", "durationMs": 0.0, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Read", "message": "289 lines from C:\\Github\\ANYopenSoft\\governance\\ECOSYSTEM_PHILOSOPHY.md", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": ""}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
