{"id": "reasoning-5a668d99-7ed0-5e9e-9e07-80d54ce3b6a9-0", "sessionId": "0c772a71-d421-e6a8-14d9-dcb034248b0b", "turnId": "turn-2f430b3805d99f342897b43671b5e559", "createdAt": 1792406871967, "updatedAt": 1792406871967, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "The GPU renderer stores `_Group(handle, ...)` with `generations` snapshot at line 1439, and at render time (line 1810) compares `current = group.handle.generations` vs `previous = group.generations` and re-uploads only changed buffers (line 2009 updates the stamp). So the GPU renderer already supports in-place generation-based updates for an existing handle: positions, displacements, scalars, transform, active, selection, appearance. That's the \"generation-based scalar updates\" mentioned in the review.\n\nSo improvement 3: in `_show_animation_frame`, instead of removing all handles and constructing new MeshHandles each frame, reuse the existing `_animation_handles` when the new frame's topology is compatible (same node count? same triangles/lines/points arrays identity/shape and chunk structure), and update the handle's mesh arrays in place via the handle's update methods. But MeshHandle._replace uses dataclasses.replace creating a new MeshArrays — fine, handle identity preserved, generations bumped, renderer detects at next render and re-uploads only changed buffers.\n\nBut wait — the animation handles have no on_change callback, so viewer._mesh_changed is not invoked; that's fine since renderer polls generations. But the viewer's `_selection_index` etc. — _show_animation_frame already resets those.\n\nCompatibility check: what counts as \"compatible topology\"? The renderer's generation sync (line 1810-2009) presumably handles position/displacement/scalar/active/selection/appearance/transform changes without re-creating buffers, but topology changes require add_mesh. Let me check what the sync does for topology generation change — likely it re-creates or marks. Let me read lines 1800-2010 of gpu/renderer.py to understand what happens on topology generation change, to define compatibility correctly.", "summary": []}
