          semantic_masks = {\r\n                \"selected_elements\": group.highlighted_elements.copy(),\r\n                \"preselected_elements\": group.preselected_elements.copy(),\r\n                \"hidden_elements\": group.hidden_elements.copy(),\r\n                \"selected_lines\": group.highlighted_lines.copy(),\r\n                \"preselected_lines\": group.preselected_lines.copy(),\r\n                \"hidden_lines\": group.hidden_lines.copy(),\r\n                \"selected_points\": group.highlighted_points.copy(),\r\n                \"preselected_points\": group.preselected_points.copy(),\r\n                \"hidden_points\": group.hidden_points.copy(),\r\n            }\r\n            chunk_semantic_masks = {\r\n                chunk_id: {\r\n                    name: values.copy() for name, values in state.items()\r\n                }\r\n                for chunk_id, state in group.chunk_semantic_masks.items()\r\n            }\r\n            chunk_pickable = set(group.chunk_pickable)\r\n            if current.selection != previous.selection:\r\n                owned_chunk_ids={chunk_id for chunk_id,_mesh,owners,_resolver in group.handle.chunk_records if owners is not None}\r\n                chunk_semantic_masks={chunk_id:state for chunk_id,state in chunk_semantic_masks.items() if chunk_id in owned_chunk_ids}\r\n            # Detach, but retain the complete previous group until both the\r\n            # replacement and its semantic state have been built successfully.\r\n            self._groups.pop(id(group.handle), None)\r\n            try:\r\n                self.add_mesh(\r\n                    group.handle,\r\n                    group.color,\r\n                    line_color=group.line_color,\r\n                    point_color=group.point_color,\r\n                    point_outline=group.point_outline,\r\n                    line_width=group.line_width,\r\n                    point_size=group.point_size,\r\n                    opacity=group.opacity,\r\n                    cull_backface=group.cull_backface,\r\n                    line_overlay=group.line_overlay,\r\n                    point_overlay=group.point_overlay,\r\n                    lit=group.lit,\r\n                    stipple=group.stipple,\r\n                    stipple_phase=group.stipple_phase,\r\n                    pickable=group.pickable,\r\n                    depth_only=group.depth_only,\r\n                    layer=group.layer,\r\n                    mesh_lines=group.mesh_lines,\r\n                    face_colors=group.face_colors,\r\n                    back_color=group.back_color,\r\n                    invalid_color=group.invalid_color,\r\n                    scalar_range=group.handle.appearance.scalar_range,\r\n                    colormap=group.handle.appearance.colormap,\r\n                )\r\n                self.set_semantic_masks(group.handle, **semantic_masks)\r\n                current_chunk_ids = {\r\n                    chunk_id for chunk_id, *_rest in group.handle.chunk_records\r\n                }\r\n                for chunk_id in chunk_pickable & current_chunk_ids:\r\n                    self.set_chunk_pickable(group.handle, chunk_id)\r\n                for chunk_id, state in chunk_semantic_masks.items():\r\n                    if chunk_id not in current_chunk_ids:\r\n                        continue\r\n                    self.set_chunk_semantic_masks(\r\n                        group.handle,\r\n                        chunk_id,\r\n                        selected_elements=state[\"highlighted_elements\"],\r\n                        preselected_elements=state[\"preselected_elements\"],\r\n                        hidden_elements=state[\"hidden_elements\"],\r\n                        selected_lines=state[\"highlighted_lines\"],\r\n                        preselected_lines=state[\"preselected_lines\"],\r\n                        hidden_lines=state[\"hidden_lines\"],\r\n                        selected_points=state[\"highlighted_points\"],\r\n                        preselected_points=state[\"preselected_points\"],\r\n                        hidden_points=state[\"hidden_points\"],\r\n                    )\r\n            except BaseException:\r\n                try:self.remove_mesh(group.handle)\r\n                except BaseException:pass  # keep the primary rebuild error\r\n                self._groups[id(group.handle)] = group\r\n                self.pick_dirty = True\r\n                raise\r\n            for _chunk_id, old_chunk in group.chunks:\r\n                self._world_bounds_cache.pop(id(old_chunk), None)\r\n                old_chunk.release()\r\n            return\r\n        sources = {chunk_id: mesh for chunk_id, mesh in [(None, group.handle.mesh), *group.handle.chunks]}\r\n        if current.displacement != previous.displacement:\r\n            # A displacement array or scale changed.  Scale is uniform-only;\r\n            # upload only if the underlying array object changed.\r\n            uploads = 0\r\n            for chunk_id, chunk in group.chunks:\r\n                mesh = sources[chunk_id]\r\n                if mesh.displacements is not chunk.mesh.displacements:\r\n                    relative = np.asarray(mesh.positions - chunk.origin, dtype=np.float32)\r\n                    displacement = (\r\n                        np.zeros_like(relative)\r\n                        if mesh.displacements is None\r\n                        else mesh.displacements.astype(np.float32, copy=False)\r\n                    )\r\n                    data = np.ascontiguousarray(\r\n                        np.column_stack((relative, displacement))\r\n                    )\r\n                    chunk.vertex_buffer.write(data.tobytes())\r\n                    if chunk.line_buffer is not None:\r\n                        chunk.line_buffer.write(_Chunk._line_data(mesh, chunk.origin).tobytes())\r\n", "numLines": 120, "startLine": 1801, "requestedOffset": 1801, "requestedLimit": 120, "totalLines": null, "wasTruncated": true}, "outputText": "", "durationMs": 0.0, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Read", "message": "120 lines from C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\src\\any3dview\\gpu\\renderer.py", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": "(truncated)"}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
{"id": "reasoning-a075453b-97da-5505-bea5-7a371032adba-0", "sessionId": "0c772a71-d421-e6a8-14d9-dcb034248b0b", "turnId": "turn-2f430b3805d99f342897b43671b5e559", "createdAt": 1792406888335, "updatedAt": 1792406888335, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "The GPU renderer's `_sync` handles generation-based updates for an existing group: topology/position changes trigger a full add_mesh rebuild (buffer recreation), while displacement/scalar/active/selection/appearance/transform changes do in-place buffer writes. So improvement 3's \"reuse handles/buffers across compatible topology\" means: in `_show_animation_frame`, keep the same MeshHandle objects across frames when topology is compatible, and update positions/displacements/scalars/transform/selected/visible via the handle's mutators. Position changes trigger renderer add_mesh rebuild though (position generation). Hmm — \"update scalars/deformation/transform\" per the task. Animation frames typically change displacements (deformation) and scalars, not base positions. So compatibility: same positions array? Actually if positions differ, renderer rebuilds anyway (position generation change → add_mesh). But handle reuse still avoids Python-side MeshHandle reconstruction and viewer-side remove/add churn... but the renderer would still rebuild buffers on position change. The task says \"reuse animation handles/buffers across compatible topology, update scalars/deformation/transform\". So the intended compatible case: topology identical (triangles, lines, point_indices, chunks structure, positions shape), and frames differ in displacements/scalars/transform/deformation_scale/selected/visible/appearance. If positions differ → treat as incompatible? Or still reuse handle but renderer rebuilds buffers. Safer: define compatible = same node count AND same triangles/lines/points arrays (by identity or equality?) AND same chunk ids AND same positions shape. For positions: if the array object differs, position generation bumps and renderer rebuilds — that's still correct, just not buffer-reuse. To keep it simple and correct: reuse handle when topology signature matches (chunk ids, triangle/line/point array shapes and content identity...). Content equality check could be expensive. Use shape + dtype? Hmm.\n\nActually, what does \"topology\" mean for buffer reuse: triangles indices, lines, point_indices, element mapping (triangle_to_element), active_elements shape, scalar shapes. The renderer's in-place paths:\n- displacement change: writes vertex_buffer with positions-displacements... uses mesh.positions relative to chunk.origin. If positions array object is the same, fine.\n- scalar change: uploads scalar texture presumably checking shape.\n- transform: uniform.\n- active: uploads active texture.\n- selection: masks.\n\nIf positions array object changes (update_positions), position generation changes → renderer rebuilds via add_mesh. That's still correct behavior (no visual defect), just less optimal. So handle reuse is safe whenever the renderer's in-place paths can consume the new arrays — but scalar texture write with different element count would fail? If element_scalars shape changes, scalar upload might break. Topology compatibility should therefore require: same triangles (shape), same lines shape, same point_indices shape, same element_count, same node count, same chunk ids. Then scalar arrays shapes are consistent.\n\nBut careful: the renderer's scalar upload path — does it check shape or just write? If element_scalars has different length than the texture, write would fail or corrupt. With same element_count it's fine.\n\nImplementation plan for improvement 3 in retained_viewer._show_animation_frame:\n- Keep `self._animation_handles` and `self._animation_entries` from the previous frame.\n- Compute a compatibility signature per item: (node_count, triangles shape + maybe bytes?, lines shape, point_indices shape, element_count, chunk ids, per-chunk signatures). Comparing triangles content each frame is O(n) but cheap relative to uploads; but np.array_equal on large index arrays each frame is some cost. Use shape + dtype + maybe hash? Simplest robust: compare shapes and use np.array_equal for index arrays? For 9712 triangles that's ~fast (microseconds-ish, 100k comparisons). Fine.\n\nActually simpler and safer: capture_animation_frame stores meshes via owned_copy(). Frames from the same capture session typically share topology. Compare:\n- len(meshes) equal and per-item: positions.shape equal, triangles shape equal AND array_equal, lines shape equal AND array_equal (or both None), point_indices likewise, element_count equal, chunk ids equal and per-chunk same checks.\n- Also triangle_to_element presence consistent.\n\nIf compatible: reuse handle: handle.update_positions? No — positions may be identical; skip if same shape and array_equal? Positions equality check is expensive for large arrays (but 3*N floats; array_equal is vectorized, fine ~ms). Hmm, but if positions differ, updating via handle.update_positions bumps position generation → renderer rebuilds buffers (add_mesh) — correct but churn. The task explicitly says \"update scalars/deformation/transform\" — so the intended reuse covers frames where positions are unchanged. I'll check positions identity (same object) — owned_copy creates new objects each capture, so identity fails. Use np.array_equal for positions too? If positions differ, we can still reuse the handle and call update_positions — renderer rebuilds buffers but keeps handle identity. That's still a win (no remove/add of all groups, no MeshHandle reconstruction, no semantic mask rebuild). But risk: renderer._sync rebuild path calls add_mesh which calls remove_mesh + create — fine.\n\nHmm wait, but there's a subtlety: the renderer's `_sync` is called during render for each group. The rebuild path preserves semantic masks. OK.\n\nBut also `_show_animation_frame` currently removes live entries' handles from renderer and clears. With reuse, we keep animation handles; live entries remain removed (they were removed when animation started... actually _show_animation_frame removes self._entries handles each call — that's also churn: removing live handles every frame even though they were already removed after the first frame. remove_mesh on a non-existent group is presumably a no-op pop. Minor.)\n\nDesign for reuse:\n```python\ndef _animation_topology_signature(item):\n    mesh = item[\"mesh\"]\n    sig = [mesh.positions.shape, None if mesh.triangles is None else mesh.triangles.shape, ...]\n```\nCompare previous frame's items to new frame's items pairwise: same count, same signature (including chunk ids and per-chunk signatures), same owners? Owner tables: item[\"owners\"] is entry owners (PackedOwnerTable) — pick identity. If owners differ between frames, the _animation_entries store owners; we must update entry owners. The entry dict fields (semantic_elements etc.) are per-frame copies — we rebuild entries each frame anyway (cheap dict copies) or update them. Simpler: rebuild `self._animation_entries` values each frame but reuse handles. Entry contains \"handle\": handle (reused), plus per-frame semantic arrays — those must reflect the new frame. So construct new entry dicts with the reused handle.\n\nHandle updates for compatible frame:\n- handle._mesh is an MeshArrays; we need to update mesh arrays: positions, displacements, node_scalars, element_scalars, active_elements. MeshHandle has update_positions, update_displacements, update_node_scalars, update_element_scalars, update_scalar_fields, set_active_elements. But no single \"replace whole mesh\" method. We could call the individual mutators only when arrays differ (by array_equal or identity). Each mutator validates and bumps generation. But these animation handles have no callback, so no viewer notification — good (renderer polls).\n\nWait — but there's a catch: MeshHandle._replace uses `replace(self._mesh, field=value)` — creates new MeshArrays. Fine.\n\nBut what about fields not covered by mutators: triangles, lines, point_indices, triangle_to_element etc.? Those are topology — compatibility requires them equal, so no update needed. But equality must be verified (array_equal) to guarantee the renderer's in-place paths are valid. If triangles differ → incompatible → full rebuild path.\n\n- transform: handle.set_transform(item[\"transform\"]) — always safe (bumps transform generation; renderer uploads uniform).\n- deformation_scale: set_deformation_scale.\n- selected: set_selected_elements.\n- visible: set_visible.\n- appearance (scalar_range/colormap): handle.update_appearance? The renderer group holds appearance from add_mesh kwargs; _sync handles appearance generation? Line 1810: names not in {\"scalar\",\"appearance\"} trigger pick_dirty. There's presumably an appearance sync path that reads handle.appearance. The group stores scalar_range/colormap from add_mesh; _sync may compare handle.appearance vs group's. Let me check further in _sync (lines 1920-2010). Also face_colors: if face_colors differ per frame, that's appearance-ish; add_mesh stores face_colors tuple. Does _sync update face_colors on appearance generation? Uncertain. To be safe: include face_colors in compatibility signature (if face_colors differ → incompatible → rebuild). Hmm, but face_colors equal check: tuple of strings, cheap.\n\nAlso \"preserve incompatible frames, chunks, owner/pick identities, restore/cancel\": \n- If any item incompatible → fall back to existing full rebuild for ALL items (simplest, preserves correctness).\n- Chunks: per-item chunk ids must match; chunk arrays topology must match; else rebuild.\n- Owner/pick identities: pick keys use id(handle) — reuse keeps stable identity across frames (previously each frame had fresh ids; but selection during animation... _selection_index is reset each frame anyway; query results would use the animation entry's owners/tags first. The fallback binding uses id(handle). With reuse, id stays constant across frames — arguably better identity preservation. The requirement \"preserve owner/pick identities\" I read as: don't break ownership resolution and pick identity semantics — reuse must keep owners/resolver/tags/item correctly bound per frame.\n- restore/cancel: stop_animation → _restore_live_renderer removes animation handles and re-adds live entries — unchanged.\n\nAlso `_show_animation_frame` sets `self._selection_index = None` each frame — keep.\n\nNow, does the renderer's _sync appearance path handle scalar_range/colormap changes? Let me check lines 1920-2010. Also need to check how scalar uploads work (shape assumptions). Let me read the rest of _sync.", "summary": []}
