       if owners is not None\r\n            }\r\n            for chunk_id in tuple(group.chunk_semantic_masks):\r\n                if chunk_id not in owned_chunk_ids:\r\n                    self.clear_chunk_semantic_masks(group.handle, chunk_id)\r\n        if current.scalar != previous.scalar:\r\n            for chunk_id, chunk in group.chunks:\r\n                mesh = sources[chunk_id]\r\n                values = (\r\n                    np.zeros(mesh.element_count, dtype=np.float32)\r\n                    if mesh.element_scalars is None\r\n                    else mesh.element_scalars.astype(np.float32, copy=False)\r\n                )\r\n                padded = np.zeros(\r\n                    max(1, chunk.element_width * math.ceil(len(values) / chunk.element_width)),\r\n                    np.float32,\r\n                )\r\n                padded[: len(values)] = values\r\n                chunk.scalars.write(padded.tobytes())\r\n                node_values = (\r\n                    np.zeros(mesh.node_count, dtype=np.float32)\r\n                    if mesh.node_scalars is None\r\n                    else mesh.node_scalars.astype(np.float32, copy=False)\r\n                )\r\n                chunk.node_scalar_buffer.write(\r\n                    np.ascontiguousarray(node_values).tobytes()\r\n                )\r\n                self.scalar_buffer_updates += 2\r\n                if chunk.node_scalar_texture is not None:\r\n                    size = chunk.node_scalar_width*max(1,math.ceil(len(node_values)/chunk.node_scalar_width))\r\n                    padded_nodes = np.zeros(size,dtype=np.float32)\r\n                    padded_nodes[:len(node_values)] = node_values\r\n                    chunk.node_scalar_texture.write(padded_nodes.tobytes())\r\n                    self.scalar_buffer_updates += 1\r\n                range_values = values if mesh.element_scalars is not None else node_values\r\n                finite = range_values[np.isfinite(range_values)]\r\n                chunk.scalar_range = (\r\n                    (float(finite.min()), float(finite.max())) if len(finite) else (0.0, 1.0)\r\n                )\r\n                chunk.has_scalars = (\r\n                    mesh.element_scalars is not None or mesh.node_scalars is not None\r\n                )\r\n                chunk.node_scalars = (\r\n                    mesh.element_scalars is None and mesh.node_scalars is not None\r\n                )\r\n        if current.appearance != previous.appearance or group.palette_dirty:\r\n            appearance=group.handle.appearance\r\n            if appearance.colormap!=group.colormap or group.palette_dirty:\r\n                data=palette_data(appearance.colormap)\r\n                rollback=palette_data(group.colormap)\r\n                try:\r\n                    for _chunk_id,chunk in group.chunks:\r\n                        chunk.colormap.write(data);self.palette_updates+=1\r\n                except BaseException:\r\n                    # A failed write may already have changed any texture.\r\n                    # Restore best-effort now and force a complete next sync;\r\n                    # preserve the primary GPU error if restoration also fails.\r\n                    group.palette_dirty=True;self.palette_write_failures+=1\r\n                    for _chunk_id,chunk in group.chunks:\r\n                        try:chunk.colormap.write(rollback);self.palette_updates+=1\r\n                        except BaseException:pass\r\n                    raise\r\n                group.palette_dirty=False\r\n            group.scalar_range=appearance.scalar_range;group.colormap=appearance.colormap\r\n            self.appearance_updates+=1\r\n        for chunk_id, chunk in group.chunks:\r\n            chunk.mesh = sources[chunk_id]\r\n        group.generations = current\r\n        visible = bool(group.handle.visible)\r\n        if visible != group.visible or any(getattr(current,name)!=getattr(previous,name) for name in current.__dataclass_fields__ if name not in {\"scalar\",\"appearance\"}):\r\n            self.pick_dirty = True\r\n        group.visible = visible\r\n\r\n    @staticmethod\r\n    def _camera_frame(\r\n        camera: Camera3D,\r\n        viewport: tuple[int, int],\r\n        section_plane: Optional[SectionPlane],\r\n    ) -> _CameraFrame:\r\n", "numLines": 100, "startLine": 1921, "requestedOffset": 1921, "requestedLimit": 100, "totalLines": null, "wasTruncated": true}, "outputText": "", "durationMs": 0.0, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Read", "message": "100 lines from C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\src\\any3dview\\gpu\\renderer.py", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": "(truncated)"}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
{"id": "reasoning-04167a82-f69c-520a-a556-b3e965933f11-0", "sessionId": "0c772a71-d421-e6a8-14d9-dcb034248b0b", "turnId": "turn-2f430b3805d99f342897b43671b5e559", "createdAt": 1792406926086, "updatedAt": 1792406926086, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "The renderer's `_sync` handles in-place updates for displacement, active, selection, scalar, appearance, transform (uniform at render), visible. Topology/position changes trigger full add_mesh rebuild. So handle reuse is safe for any change as long as the handle's arrays remain self-consistent — the renderer rebuilds on position/topology generation change. But scalar uploads assume element_count consistent with chunk.element_width — element_count comes from mesh; if topology same, fine.\n\nImportant: the scalar path writes `chunk.scalars` sized by element_count — if a compatible frame has same element_count, fine.\n\nSo compatibility signature for handle reuse (avoiding renderer rebuild churn): require positions array_equal? Actually if positions differ, renderer rebuilds buffers — still correct. The task says \"reuse animation handles/buffers across compatible topology, update scalars/deformation/transform\". So compatible topology = same triangles/lines/points/chunks. Positions: if positions differ but topology same — the renderer rebuild path handles it correctly (add_mesh). But is that \"reuse\"? Handle reused, buffers rebuilt. I'll include positions in the signature as \"must be array_equal\" to guarantee buffer reuse; if positions differ → treat as incompatible → full rebuild path (which is exactly today's behavior). Hmm, but that could make reuse never trigger if positions vary per frame. Animation frames from ANYfem: positions are typically the same base mesh; deformation in displacements. So positions array_equal will hold. But array_equal on every frame for every mesh costs O(N) — trivial vs upload costs.\n\nActually wait — there's a subtlety: comparing with np.array_equal each frame for large position arrays (e.g., 20k triangles → ~10k vertices → 30k floats) is sub-millisecond. Fine.\n\nBut also: what about `triangle_to_element`, `element_ids`, `line_to_element`? Those are topology-ish; if they differ, renderer rebuild is triggered only by topology/position generation. If we don't update them (handle keeps old mesh's mapping) but new frame's semantics (owners) reference new element ids... The _animation_entries store owners from the item; if triangle_to_element differs between frames while triangles shape is same, reusing the handle would keep the OLD mapping → wrong pick identity. So signature must include: triangles, lines, point_indices, triangle_to_element, line_to_element, element_ids, active_elements? active is updatable via set_active_elements (generation 'active', in-place upload). element_scalars/node_scalars shapes must match element_count/node_count — guaranteed if topology matches... element_scalars length = element_count presumably. If a frame has element_scalars of different length than element_count, validation would have failed at capture. OK.\n\nSimplest robust signature: compare the topology-bearing fields with np.array_equal (or both None): positions, triangles, lines, point_indices, triangle_to_element, line_to_element, element_ids, element_scalars presence shape? No — scalars are updated in place; shape must match element_count which is fixed. But node_scalars length must equal node_count — fixed. OK.\n\nAlso chunk structure: same chunk ids, and per-chunk same topology fields. And face_colors: group stores face_colors tuple from add_mesh; _sync doesn't update face_colors (not in the shown code; appearance path only handles colormap/scalar_range). So if face_colors differ per frame, reuse would show stale face colors. Include face_colors in signature (tuple equality — cheap). Also other appearance kwargs (color, opacity, etc.) are stored in group at add_mesh; _sync doesn't update them except scalar_range/colormap. So if item[\"appearance\"] differs in any key other than scalar_range/colormap, reuse would be stale. Include appearance (minus scalar_range/colormap) in signature; if differs → rebuild that... or whole frame. Per-item granularity: if any item incompatible → rebuild everything (simplest, matches \"preserve incompatible frames\"). Actually per-item reuse would be better but more complex; the task says \"preserve incompatible frames\" — I'll do per-frame all-or-nothing? Hmm, \"reuse animation handles/buffers across compatible topology... preserve incompatible frames, chunks, owner/pick identities\". I'll implement per-item compatibility: items pair with previous handles by index; if counts differ or any item incompatible → full rebuild of all. That's simplest and safest.\n\nWait, one more: `handle.update_appearance` for scalar_range/colormap changes — reuse path should call handle.update_appearance(scalar_range=..., colormap=...) so renderer picks up new palette. And set_visible/set_transform/set_deformation_scale/set_selected_elements.\n\nFor mesh array updates on reuse: compare arrays by identity first (owned_copy makes new arrays each capture, so identity fails across frames) then np.array_equal. Update only differing fields via the handle mutators:\n- positions: if not array_equal → update_positions (bumps position gen → renderer rebuilds; still correct). Actually if positions differ, maybe just treat as incompatible to guarantee buffer reuse? The renderer handles position change correctly via rebuild. I'll allow it: update_positions is correct. But then \"buffers\" aren't reused for that frame — fine, correctness first.\n- displacements: update_displacements if differ (or one None).\n- node_scalars/element_scalars: update via update_scalar_fields if either differs.\n- active_elements: set_active_elements if differ.\n\nBut careful: MeshHandle._replace validates via self._validate_mesh — animation handles created without validate_mesh? In _show_animation_frame: MeshHandle(item[\"mesh\"], scalar_range=..., colormap=...) — no on_change, no validate. So updates won't trigger viewer callbacks. Good — but wait, should the reused handle have the viewer's _mesh_changed as callback? No — animation handles never had it. Keep none.\n\nHmm, but there's a wrinkle: handle._replace uses `replace(self._mesh, ...)` — the MeshArrays dataclass. Fine.\n\nNow — the Qt _show_animation_frame override calls make_current then super. Fine.\n\nAlso `_restore_live_renderer` unchanged.\n\nEdge: `begin_animation_cache` clears cache; stop_animation restores. When animation restarts (play_animation after stop), _animation_handles cleared by _restore_live_renderer. First _show_animation_frame builds handles; subsequent frames reuse. Also when index jumps (scrubbing), reuse still valid since compatibility checked per frame.\n\nNow improvement 2: software camera frame retained preparation/cache. _paint_software in qt.py does per-frame: world = positions @ transform (camera-independent!), masks sets, scalar colors, appearance dicts, section clipping of lines (camera-independent), triangle admission (active/hidden — camera-independent), colors per triangle (camera-independent except highlight). The camera-dependent parts: projection (screen coords), depth sorting, painter submission.\n\nSo design a per-(handle,chunk) retained preparation cache storing:\n- world positions array\n- projected admission: list of (index, element, color) for triangles? Colors depend on highlight masks (selected_elements, masks) which change with selection — invalidation via generations.\n- lines: world segments (Point3D pairs) after section clipping — camera-independent.\n- points: world positions.\n- scalar colors per element.\n\nInvalidation: handle generations (topology, position, displacement, active, selection, appearance, transform), masks (semantic masks set via _apply_highlight_masks → renderer.set_semantic_masks / set_highlighted_elements), section plane, show_mesh_lines, visibility. The existing `_selection_key`-style generation tuple can serve as cache key per handle.\n\nWhere to store? qt.py owns _paint_software; but retained_viewer.py is also owned. The Tk host also paints software? Let me check: does retained_viewer have a software paint path used by Tk? grep \"_paint_software\" — it's defined in qt.py only. Tk host (hosts.py?) probably has its own. Our scope: qt.py + retained_viewer.py. The cache can live in qt.py as a private helper class `_Software Preparation` keyed by (id(handle), chunk_id) with a signature. But masks live in self._renderer.masks (SoftwareResources). Invalidation of masks: _apply_highlight_masks calls renderer.set_semantic_masks/set_highlighted_elements — those go to _SoftwareResources (software) or GPU renderer. For software, _SoftwareResources.set_semantic_masks just stores. We can bump a revision counter in _SoftwareResources on each mask mutation, and include it in the cache signature. _SoftwareResources is in qt.py — owned. \n\nAlso appearance changes: entry[\"appearance\"] dict mutated by _mesh_changed('appearance') — includes generation bump on handle. Handle generations capture appearance changes (set_visible bumps 'appearance'; update_appearance bumps 'appearance'). Entry appearance dict itself: _mesh_changed updates scalar_range/colormap in entry['appearance'] — handle.appearance also changes (generation 'appearance'). OK.\n\nBut entry[\"appearance\"] can also be mutated by other paths? e.g., set_highlight? Let me not over-engineer: signature per (handle, chunk):\n(id(handle), chunk_id, handle.generations (excluding scalar? no — scalar affects colors! include all), masks revision for that handle/chunk, section plane key+enabled, show_mesh_lines flag, appearance dict content? appearance dict is entry[\"appearance\"] — include a snapshot? The dict is mutated in place (scalar_range/colormap). handle.generations.appearance bumps on update_appearance. But _mesh_changed('appearance') updates entry['appearance']['scalar_range'/'colormap'] from handle.appearance — same generation. Other appearance keys (color, opacity...) are set at add time only. So generations + masks revision suffice... but wait: `entry[\"appearance\"]` gets scalar_range/colormap from handle.appearance in _paint_software anyway (`dict(entry[\"appearance\"], scalar_range=..., colormap=...)`).\n\nHmm — but there's also `handle.selected_elements` (set_selected_elements bumps 'selection' generation) and masks. And `_highlight_fill` (viewer-level) affects highlighted triangle color — include viewer._highlight_fill and _preselected? The paint uses self._highlight_fill for highlighted and \"#ffd166\" for preselected. Include (self._highlight_fill,) in signature... preselected color is constant. Also `self.pixel_ratio` affects pen widths at submission (camera-frame constant per paint; not per-handle cache). Depth sorting happens at submission from cached per-triangle depth — depth depends on camera. So cache stores world-space data + per-triangle (element, color) and screen projection computed per frame.\n\nWhat exactly to cache per (handle, chunk):\n1. world: Nx3 array (positions + deformation_scale*displacements) @ transform\n2. triangle admissions: array of triangle indices admitted (active, not hidden) — but hidden depends on masks (revision). Also per-triangle color string (highlight/scalar/face_colors) — depends on masks + scalars + appearance.\n3. lines: list of (a_world, b_world) after section clip? Section clipping per line: `segment=self._section_plane.clip_segment(...)` — camera-independent. Cache list of world-space segment pairs (as tuples/arrays).\n4. points: world positions admitted (hidden_points, section contains).\n5. outline groups: entry[\"outline_groups\"] — _surface_outline_segments computes from world + triangles + frame. It projects... let me look at _surface_outline_segments (line 3363). It takes (entry, chunk_id, mesh, triangles, world, frame) and returns screen segments — projection inside. The camera-independent part is the loop extraction; projection is camera-dependent. Caching outline loops (world-space) would help. Let me read that function.\n\nAlso scalar_limits computation per chunk — cacheable.\n\nThis is getting big. Given budget (MaxTurns40, checkpoint ~8 turns), I need to be pragmatic: implement a retained software preparation cache in qt.py storing per-(handle,chunk) camera-independent prepared data, invalidated by a signature (generations, masks revision, section key, show_mesh_lines, highlight fill, appearance identity). Per frame: compute signature per handle (cheap), reuse prepared data, project with frame, submit to painter identically.\n\nPixel identity: the projection math and submission order must be exactly the same. The current code computes `world` then `screen_all, visible_all = self._project_world_points(world, frame)`, `projected` list, triangle_visible, etc. If I cache world and admission lists, the per-frame work is: project world (needed per frame — camera-dependent), then for each admitted triangle get screen = screen_all[triangle], compute color from cached per-triangle color list, depth = mean of screen z. Same numbers → identical pixels.\n\nFor lines: current code iterates mesh.lines, clips segment, projects each endpoint via _project_point_with_frame. Cache the world-space clipped segments (Point3D pairs) and index/selected flag; per frame project endpoints. Identical math.\n\nFor points: cache world positions + hidden/selected; per frame project.\n\nFor outlines: _surface_outline_segments — need to read it. It uses visible_triangles (admitted indices) and world and frame; computes boundary loops then projects. The loop extraction (which edges are boundaries between admitted triangles) is camera-independent given admitted set. Let me read lines 3319-3432.\n\nAlso the section!=None path per triangle clips polygons in world — camera-independent (clip_polygon uses world points). Cache clipped world polygons per triangle? That's the slow path; caching them is fine too (world-space). But `verts = np.array([p.to_tuple() for p in section.clip_polygon(world[triangle])])` — cache the resulting world verts array per triangle; per frame project. Good.\n\nLet me read _surface_outline_segments and _project_world_points/_project_point_with_frame and _FrameProjection to plan precisely.", "summary": []}
