{"id": "effect-eb388a1d-ad68-5a04-ab33-e09e06db723b", "sessionId": "0c772a71-d421-e6a8-14d9-dcb034248b0b", "turnId": "turn-2f430b3805d99f342897b43671b5e559", "createdAt": 1792406926114, "updatedAt": 1792406926187, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "effect", "title": "file_system.read_file", "detail": {"toolName": "file_system.read_file", "display": {"summary": "Reading C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\src\\any3dview\\retained_viewer.py", "content": null, "suffix": "", "verb": "Reading", "message": "C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\src\\any3dview\\retained_viewer.py", "settledVerb": "Read", "settledMessage": "C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\src\\any3dview\\retained_viewer.py", "statusText": "Reading file"}, "kind": "file_read", "input": {"filePath": "C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\src\\any3dview\\retained_viewer.py", "offset": 369, "limit": 50}}, "state": {"status": "completed", "output": {"filePath": "C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\src\\any3dview\\retained_viewer.py", "content": "        value = as_point(point)\r\n        width, height = self.viewport_size\r\n        projected = self.camera.project_point(value, width, height)\r\n        if projected is None:\r\n            return None\r\n        _right, _up, forward = self.camera.basis()\r\n        depth = (value - self.camera.position).dot(forward)\r\n        return float(projected[0]), float(projected[1]), float(depth)\r\n\r\n\r\n    def _project_world_points(self, world, frame):\r\n        \"\"\"Vectorized project_point over a world-space point array.\r\n\r\n        Returns ``(screen, visible)`` where screen rows are ``(x, y, depth)``\r\n        and visible marks rows strictly between the camera's near and far\r\n        planes, matching project_point's ``None`` contract.\r\n        \"\"\"\r\n        camera = (np.asarray(world, dtype=float).reshape((-1, 3)) - frame.origin) @ frame.basis.T\r\n        depth = camera[:, 2]\r\n        visible = (depth > frame.near) & (depth < frame.far)\r\n        safe = np.where(visible, depth, 1.0)\r\n        x = (camera[:, 0] * frame.scale / frame.aspect / safe + 1.0) * 0.5 * frame.width\r\n        y = (1.0 - camera[:, 1] * frame.scale / safe) * 0.5 * frame.height\r\n        return np.stack((x, y, depth), axis=1), visible\r\n\r\n    def _project_point_with_frame(self, point, frame):\r\n        \"\"\"Single-point projection reusing frame-local camera state.\"\"\"\r\n        value = as_point(point)\r\n        camera = (np.asarray(value.to_tuple()) - frame.origin) @ frame.basis.T\r\n        depth = float(camera[2])\r\n        if depth <= frame.near or depth >= frame.far:\r\n            return None\r\n        return (\r\n            float((camera[0] * frame.scale / frame.aspect / depth + 1.0) * 0.5 * frame.width),\r\n            float((1.0 - camera[1] * frame.scale / depth) * 0.5 * frame.height),\r\n            depth,\r\n        )\r\n\r\n    def project_points(\r\n        self, points: Iterable[object]\r\n    ) -> tuple[Optional[tuple[float, float, float]], ...]:\r\n        return tuple(self.project_point(point) for point in points)\r\n\r\n\r\n    def screen_ray(self, x: float, y: float) -> tuple[Point3D, Point3D]:\r\n        width, height = self.viewport_size\r\n        return self.camera.screen_ray(x, y, width, height)\r\n\r\n\r\n    def unproject_to_plane(\r\n", "numLines": 50, "startLine": 369, "requestedOffset": 369, "requestedLimit": 50, "totalLines": null, "wasTruncated": true}, "outputText": "", "durationMs": 0.0, "display": {"success": true, "verb": "Read", "message": "50 lines from C:\\Github\\ANY3dView\\src\\any3dview\\retained_viewer.py", "warnings": [], "approvalNote": null, "suffix": "(truncated)"}, "decision": "execute", "approvalType": "always", "approvalSource": "bypass"}}
{"id": "reasoning-49ceb576-93eb-590b-b708-0d595b08538d-0", "sessionId": "0c772a71-d421-e6a8-14d9-dcb034248b0b", "turnId": "turn-2f430b3805d99f342897b43671b5e559", "createdAt": 1792406997128, "updatedAt": 1792406997128, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "I now have a good picture. Given the scale, I need to be pragmatic about improvement 2. The heaviest per-camera-frame invariant work in `_paint_software`:\n\n1. `world = positions @ transform.T + t` — matrix multiply per frame (camera-independent).\n2. masks set construction (hidden/highlighted/preselected per chunk) — camera-independent.\n3. scalar_limits computation — camera-independent.\n4. per-triangle admission loop (active/hidden) and color computation — camera-independent.\n5. line section clipping + Point3D construction — camera-independent.\n6. point admission — camera-independent.\n7. `_surface_outline_segments` — the loop extraction `_surface_outline_loops(mesh, triangles, groups)` is camera-independent given admitted triangles; projection is camera-dependent. Also the fast path check `depths.min() > near and depths.max() < far` is camera-dependent.\n\nDesign: a `_SoftwareFramePrep` cache in qt.py: dict keyed (id(handle), chunk_id) → prepared record + signature. Signature: (handle.generations, masks-revision for handle/chunk, section plane key/enabled, show_mesh_lines, highlight_fill, appearance scalar_range/colormap identity, face_colors identity...). Prepared record holds:\n- world (Nx3 float64)\n- triangle indices admitted (np array), per-triangle color strings (list), and for section path: per-triangle clipped world verts? The section path in _paint_software: `verts = np.array([p.to_tuple() for p in section.clip_polygon(world[triangle])])` then projects. Cache verts per admitted triangle when section enabled.\n- lines: list of ((ax,ay,az),(bx,by,bz)) world segments post-section-clip, plus selected flag per line (style line_color override at submission).\n- points: world coords + selected flag.\n- outline loops: list of loops (index tuples) from _surface_outline_loops — cache the loops (node indices) given admitted triangles; per frame the fast path projects world[loop]... wait the fast path projects all world points then indexes screen by loop indices. The loops are index-based, so cache loops only.\n\nHmm — but the depth-range check for the outline fast path is camera-dependent, so per frame we compute depths and choose path. With loops cached, both paths work: fast path uses screen_all[loop indices]; slow path uses _project_outline_segment(world[list(edge)]).\n\nBut careful about exact pixel identity: current code calls `self._surface_outline_segments(entry, chunk_id, mesh, visible_triangles, world, frame)` which internally recomputes `_outline_groups` validation (mutating entry[\"outline_groups\"] to None if stale). If I cache loops, I must reproduce the same behavior including the entry mutation. I can call `_surface_outline_loops` during preparation (cache miss) and keep the `_outline_groups` validation call.\n\nAlso the \"cut\" sections at the end of _surface_outline_segments iterate triangles with plane/depth cuts — camera-dependent (depth limits). Leave that per-frame, using cached world.\n\nSimplification decision: I'll restructure `_paint_software` to use a per-(handle,chunk) preparation cache with signature-based invalidation, keeping all projection and submission code identical. The prepared record:\n\n```python\nclass _ChunkPrep:\n    __slots__ = (\"signature\",\"world\",\"triangles\",\"colors\",\"section_verts\",\"lines\",\"points\",\"loops\", ...)\n```\n\nPer-frame flow per (handle, chunk):\n- signature = (handle.generations, mask_revision(handle,chunk_id), section_key, show_mesh_lines, highlight_fill, appearance_style...)\n- if signature differs → rebuild prep.\n- projection per frame: screen_all, visible_all = _project_world_points(world, frame) (needed anyway for triangle_visible and points).\n- triangles: for each admitted triangle index: if section: screen from cached section_verts projected via _project_world_points(verts) — wait current code: `screen, visible = self._project_world_points(verts, frame)`; `if len(screen)<3 or not visible.all(): continue`. Then faces.append(depth mean...). Cache verts; per frame project. Same math.\n  - else: `if not triangle_visible[index]: continue; screen = screen_all[triangle]`. triangle_visible computed per frame from visible_all (camera-dependent). Keep per frame.\n- colors cached per admitted triangle.\n- lines: cached world segments; per frame `a,b=(self._project_point_with_frame(Point3D(*seg_a), frame)...)`. Current code constructs `segment=(Point3D(*world[a]),Point3D(*world[b]))` then clip then projects with `_project_point_with_frame(p, frame)` where p is Point3D. If section is None, clip_segment isn't applied (`if self._section_plane is not None:segment=self._section_plane.clip_segment(*segment)`). Note: it checks `self._section_plane is not None` not `enabled`! While `section` variable uses enabled. Careful: in _paint_software, `section` is the enabled plane, but the line path checks `self._section_plane is not None` (even disabled!). And the point path checks `self._section_plane is not None and not contains`. Hmm — a disabled section plane still clips lines/points in the current code? clip_segment on a disabled plane — does SectionPlane.clip_segment check enabled? Let me check clipping.py. If clip_segment respects enabled internally, then behavior is same. I must preserve exact behavior — cache the result of the same operations: for lines, apply the same `if self._section_plane is not None: segment=clip_segment(...)` during preparation. But preparation is cached across frames; section plane state is in signature (section.key includes enabled? `_section_plane.key` — need to check). If key doesn't include enabled, include enabled flag.\n\nActually wait — there's a subtlety: preparation happens on cache miss; the section plane object could be mutated in place (set_section_plane replaces? `set_section_plane` at line 2929 — probably replaces self._section_plane with a new object or mutates). Signature includes `None if plane is None else plane.key` — does key capture position/enable? Let me check SectionPlane.key in clipping.py.\n\n- points: cached world positions + admission; per frame: `if projected[i] is None: continue` — projected is per-frame from screen_all/visible_all. And section contains check — camera-independent, cache admission.\n\nOK. And the masks revision: _SoftwareResources needs a revision counter bumped on set_semantic_masks, set_highlighted_elements, set_chunk_semantic_masks, clear_chunk_semantic_masks, remove_mesh (removes masks), add_mesh. Since _paint_software reads `self._renderer.masks` and `chunk_masks`, and those mutate through those methods, add `self.revision = 0` bumped in each mutator. But _SoftwareResources is also used... only for software. GPU renderer has its own. But careful: tests may construct fake renderers. The paint code uses getattr for safety? I'll use `getattr(self._renderer, \"mask_revision\", 0)`.\n\nAlso `handle.selected_elements` — part of handle.generations ('selection'). Good.\n\nAppearance: entry[\"appearance\"] — scalar_range/colormap updated on 'appearance' change (generation bump). Other keys static. But `dict(entry[\"appearance\"], scalar_range=handle.appearance.scalar_range, colormap=...)` — the paint builds appearance per chunk from entry + handle.appearance. handle.generations.appearance covers it. face_colors: static per entry (part of appearance dict, set at add). OK — but wait, is entry[\"appearance\"] ever replaced wholesale? _mesh_changed('appearance') mutates scalar_range/colormap keys only. add_mesh_arrays creates entry. So generations cover everything except masks (revision) and... `entry.get(\"outline_groups\")` — set at add; `_outline_groups` may set entry[\"outline_groups\"]=None when stale (mutation during paint!). That mutation is camera-independent; happens inside _surface_outline_segments. If I cache loops, I should perform the same validation+mutation during preparation.\n\nAlso `self.show_mesh_lines` — in signature. `self._highlight_fill` — viewer-level; affects colors of highlighted triangles and selected lines/points. Include in signature. `appearance[\"mesh_lines\"]` per entry — static.\n\nAlso `handle.deformation_scale` — in generations ('displacement'). transform — 'transform'. \n\nNow the risk: signature misses something → stale cache → wrong pixels. Tests will cover invalidation: topology/position/displacement/scalar/active/selection/appearance/transform changes, masks (highlight), section plane, show_mesh_lines, visibility (handle.visible — checked before cache lookup; removed/visible skip), highlight_fill.\n\nEdge: `entry[\"appearance\"]` includes \"depth_only\" — checked before chunk loop; static.\n\nNow improvement 1: GPU hover coalescing. Currently in _InputSurface.mouseMoveEvent: unpressed GPU moves call `self.owner._hover_select(e)` synchronously per event. Improvement: coalesce to one latest event per frame. Implementation: for GPU backend, store pending hover (latest event) and schedule delivery at next frame — via a queued notification tied to render. Options: use `self.update()` + deliver hover in paintGL? That couples hover with paint. Or use QTimer.singleShot(0)? That's per-event-loop iteration, not per frame. \"coalesce optional GPU hover latest event per frame\" — one hover evaluation per rendered frame. Simplest robust: pending latest event + QTimer.singleShot(0, deliver) — coalesces multiple moves within one event-loop iteration into one hover at end. But \"per frame\" — during camera drag hover is canceled anyway. Moves without paint in between... GPU hover triggers preselection change → redraw. A 0-shot timer coalesces bursts of mouse moves queued before paint. Actually Qt processes all queued mouse events then timers? Qt compresses mouse moves anyway. The plan says \"coalesce optional hover to one latest request per frame; preserve exact clicks and stale-camera cancellation\". \n\nDesign: reuse the existing `_queue_hover`/`_cancel_hover`/`_deliver_hover` machinery but with a frame-scoped mode for GPU: latest-event-wins with a single-shot 0ms timer (next event loop pass), canceled by press/wheel/drag/leave/hide/focus-out (already calling _cancel_hover). Clicks: mousePressEvent calls _cancel_hover then _press_select — exact click dispatch unchanged (clicks don't go through hover). Stale events canceled: _deliver_hover checks surface identity, underMouse, drag state — for GPU also check that the pending event's... \"stale events canceled\" — cancel on camera motion (buttons pressed → _motion path already cancels). \n\nBut careful: GPU hover previously was synchronous — hover feedback latency now up to one event-loop turn. Acceptable per plan (\"optional hover\").\n\nImplementation: in mouseMoveEvent, replace `self.owner._hover_select(e)` with `self.owner._queue_hover(e, immediate=...)`? The software path uses 120ms quiet debounce. For GPU use 0ms coalescing: `_queue_hover(e, delay=0)`? Modify _queue_hover to accept interval; for GPU call with 0. QTimer.start(0) fires on next event loop iteration — coalesces all moves queued in the current batch into one delivery with the latest position. That is \"one latest per frame\"-ish. Hmm, \"per frame\" strictly means tie to render. Alternative: deliver pending hover at the start of paintGL (before render? after?). If no repaint occurs (hover doesn't change → no redraw), pending hover would sit. But hover that changes preselection triggers redraw; if hover result is same key, no redraw needed and pending cleared after delivery. If we only deliver in paintGL, first hover ever needs a paint — GPU widget paints on show/update. Risky: if no paint is scheduled, hover never delivers. Combine: schedule singleShot(0) delivery AND that's it. I'll go with 0ms timer coalescing; it's bounded, no lost wakeup, and stale-cancelable. The plan's acceptance: \"regression tests for invalidation and refusal\" — tests: multiple moves → one hover call with latest coords; click during pending → hover canceled, click exact; drag cancels; close cancels.\n\nWait — but there's a subtlety with `_deliver_hover`'s checks: `surface.underMouse()` — for GPU moves, underMouse is true. OK.\n\nAlso `_hover_select` on GPU calls query_point which uses cached GPU ID target (pick_detail reads framebuffer synchronously — the stall). Coalescing reduces frequency to ≤1 per event-loop pass.\n\nImprovement 5: queued thread-safe scheduler notification replacing idle 2ms poll. Currently QtViewer._start_update_pump creates a QTimer(2ms) polling _update_scheduler.drain(). Replace with event-queue notification: scheduler gets a `notify` hook — when submit() is called (from any thread), it invokes a callback that queues an event on the owner thread. In Qt: use QCoreApplication.postEvent to a QObject that drains. Design:\n\n- scheduler.py: add optional `notifier` — `ViewerScheduler(notify=None)`? But scheduler is shared with Tk (\"Tk compatible\"). Tk host uses `after(2, _poll_updates)` loop (retained_viewer._poll_updates). Requirement: \"queued thread-safe Qt scheduler notification replacing idle 2ms poll, bounded1024 FIFO drain, no lost wakeup, close and failures safe, Tk compatible.\"\n\nDesign: add to ViewerScheduler a thread-safe `set_notifier(callback)`/notification mechanism: submit() puts item in queue then calls `self._notify()` if set. The notifier must be thread-safe (callable from any thread). For Qt: a QObject with custom event posted via QCoreApplication.postEvent (thread-safe). The QObject lives on owner thread; its event handler calls viewer._poll_updates → drain(1024) → redraw if any.\n\nNo lost wakeup: notification posted on every submit while queue non-empty; drain drains up to 1024; if more remain, drain again? Bounded 1024 FIFO drain per notification; if items remain after 1024, post another notification (or loop drain until empty with 1024 cap per batch? \"bounded1024 FIFO drain\" — cap per drain at 1024; to avoid lost items, after draining 1024, if queue non-empty, re-post notification). SimpleQueue is thread-safe; submit after close raises RuntimeError. close(): set closed, clear queue; late submit raises. Notification after close must be safe (posted event checks closed).\n\nQt side: replace `_start_update_pump`'s 2ms timer with:\n- initial drain (as before)\n- create a `_UpdateNotifier(QObject)` owned by viewer; scheduler.set_notifier(lambda: QCoreApplication.postEvent(notifier, QEvent(_update_event_type))) — postEvent is thread-safe and queues on the receiver's thread.\n- notifier.customEvent → if not closed: completed = drain(); if completed: redraw(); and if scheduler has pending items remaining (drain hit the 1024 bound), repost.\n\nWait — but drain is also called... the retained_viewer._poll_updates (Tk path) uses after(2) rescheduling. QtViewer overrides _poll_updates (no reschedule). With queued notification, Qt no longer needs the timer at all. \"replacing idle 2ms poll\" — remove the persistent QTimer. Keep `_update_poll_id` semantics? Tests test_qt_update_pump.py exist and assert timer behavior! \"focused tests\" — I own tests; existing tests/test_qt_update_pump.py and test_scheduler.py are preexisting dirty (untracked). The task says owned files include \"focused tests only; preserve preexisting edits\". Existing test files test_qt_update_pump.py assert the 2ms pump behavior — my change would break them. I own tests — I can update those focused tests to the new contract (they're part of the five-improvement slice; the pump test file is one of \"focused tests\"). The task says \"Owned src/any3dview/qt.py, retained_viewer.py, scheduler.py and focused tests only\" — so updating tests/test_qt_update_pump.py and tests/test_scheduler.py is in scope.\n\nScheduler changes (scheduler.py owned):\n```python\ndef __init__(self, notifier=None): ... self._notifier = notifier (callable or None)\ndef set_notifier(self, callback): self._notifier = callback\ndef submit(...): queue.put(...); then call notifier safely:\n    notifier = self._notifier\n    if notifier is not None:\n        try: notifier()\n        except Exception: pass?? \n```\nHmm \"failures safe\" — if notifier fails (e.g., Qt object deleted), we must not crash the submitting worker thread? Or should failure propagate? \"close and failures safe\" — a notifier failure shouldn't lose the item (it's already queued) nor crash. But silently swallowing means no wakeup → lost wakeup until next submit. For Qt postEvent to a deleted QObject — postEvent to deleted object is safe? If receiver deleted, event is discarded... QCoreApplication.postEvent requires the receiver to be a QObject; if it's being deleted, Qt handles it (events for deleted objects are discarded). To be safe, wrap notifier call in try/except and on failure fall back? Keep simple: catch Exception, ignore (item remains queued; next submit retries; drain also happens on redraw paths). Also drain() itself: after draining, if queue still non-empty (hit limit), call notifier again to schedule continued drain.\n\nAlso thread-safety of set_notifier: called on owner thread at setup; submit may race from other threads — reading self._notifier is atomic enough (GIL). Fine.\n\nTk compatibility: default notifier None → behavior unchanged (Tk keeps its after(2) poll loop via retained_viewer._poll_updates). \n\nQt wiring: in QtViewer.__init__ after backend probe: `self._start_update_pump()` → rename/replace with `_start_update_queue()`:\n```python\ndef _start_update_pump(self):\n    if self._closed or self._update_poll_id is not None: return\n    self._poll_updates()  # initial drain\n    if self._closed: return\n    app = QCoreApplication.instance()  # must exist\n    notifier = _UpdateNotifier(self)  # QObject child\n    self._update_notifier = notifier\n    self._update_scheduler.set_notifier(lambda: QCoreApplication.postEvent(notifier, _UpdateEvent()))\n```\nBut wait: QCoreApplication.instance() may be None if no app — QtViewer requires QApplication anyway (QWidget). Fine.\n\nCustom event: need QEvent.Type registration: `_UPDATE_EVENT = QEvent.Type(QEvent.registerEventType())` at module import — registerEventType is static thread-safe? It's fine at module level.\n\n_UpdateNotifier(QObject): customEvent(self, event): if event.type() == _UPDATE_EVENT: owner=self._owner; if owner._closed: return; completed = owner._update_scheduler.drain(); if completed: owner.redraw(); if owner._update_scheduler has pending: repost... Actually drain returns count; pending check: add `scheduler.pending()` → bool not empty. If pending after drain (hit 1024 bound), post again.\n\nHmm, but \"no lost wakeup\": sequence — worker submits item, notifier posts event; owner drains; meanwhile worker submits another item after drain started → notifier posts again → second event drains it. If worker submits during drain and the item is drained by the ongoing drain (get_nowait), then the posted event finds empty queue → drain returns 0 → no redraw. Fine, no loss.\n\nRace: submit puts item, then notifier called; if notifier is None (not yet set), no wakeup — but initial drain happens before set_notifier, and set_notifier happens in constructor before any submit? Other threads could submit between viewer construction... submit_update is public; ANYfem worker threads submit during construction? The scheduler exists from initialize_state; set_notifier at end of __init__. A submit before set_notifier would sit until next submit or initial drain. To be safe: after set_notifier, do one more drain+redraw? Or set notifier BEFORE surface creation... but notifier QObject needs... QObject can be created anytime; postEvent needs QCoreApplication existing. Do: create notifier + set_notifier FIRST in _start_update_pump, then initial drain. Then any submit after that gets notified. Submits between scheduler creation (initialize_state) and set_notifier: only possible if user code submits during QtViewer.__init__ — unlikely; and initial drain catches those before... no wait, order: set_notifier first, then initial drain: items submitted before set_notifier are caught by initial drain. Items submitted after set_notifier get events. \n\nAlso destroy(): close scheduler (close() clears queue, sets closed; late submit raises RuntimeError — that's existing contract). The notifier QObject: parented to viewer → deleted with viewer; posted events to deleted objects are discarded by Qt. Also must handle customEvent after close → checks owner._closed. And set_notifier(None) on destroy? scheduler.close() then notifier may still fire → postEvent → customEvent → owner._closed → return. Safe.\n\nAlso `_poll_updates` in QtViewer — keep for compatibility (initial drain + tests), but the timer goes away. `_update_poll_id` — retained_viewer sets it in Tk path; Qt path previously stored the QTimer. Now set `self._update_poll_id = None`... it's used as \"pump started\" guard. I'll keep a guard flag using _update_poll_id = notifier object.\n\nNow improvement 4: `_selection_key` — replace `entry[\"handle\"].generations` with a narrowed tuple excluding scalar and appearance generations? Wait — appearance generation covers set_visible (visible already separately in key) and update_appearance (scalar_range/colormap — scalar-only visual). But _mesh_changed('appearance') with scalar_appearance=False sets `self._selection_index = None` anyway. The key's job: detect changes since last index build. Excluding scalar+appearance from key: scalar changes don't affect projected geometry (colors only) — correct per plan (\"exclude scalar-only changes but preserve all geometry/visibility/ownership invalidation\"). Ownership: 'selection' generation covers set_chunk_ownership and set_selected_elements — ownership changes bump 'selection' → keep selection generation in key. Visibility: handle.visible in key; visibility_state in key; masks? The projected index uses hidden elements from masks? Let me check what _projected_selection_index consumes — it iterates display entries and meshes; does it use renderer masks (hidden_elements)? Let me check the middle of _projected_selection_index (lines 1490-1700). It probably uses _semantic_masks_for_mesh / hidden primitives from visibility state. If it uses renderer masks, then mask changes (highlight) must invalidate — highlight masks are applied via _apply_highlight_masks → renderer.set_semantic_masks; does that reset _selection_index? Hidden elements affect picking. Let me read that section quickly.\n\nAlso note: `_selection_index = None` is set by _mesh_changed for non-scalar changes, so the key is a second guard. The plan: \"projected selection cache exclude scalar-only changes but preserve all geometry/visibility/ownership invalidation\". So modify _selection_key to use a narrowed generations tuple: (topology, position, displacement, active, selection, transform) — excluding scalar and appearance. But wait: appearance generation also covers set_visible — visible is already in the key explicitly. OK.\n\nBut does the projected index depend on appearance at all (e.g., pickable flag, depth_only)? If entry appearance has pickable=False, index may skip. Appearance dict static except scalar_range/colormap. Fine.\n\nLet me read _projected_selection_index body to confirm dependencies (masks usage).", "summary": []}
