{"id": "reasoning-8a799b37-8247-5118-aeec-8e1e93574d61-0", "sessionId": "0c772a71-d421-e6a8-14d9-dcb034248b0b", "turnId": "turn-2f430b3805d99f342897b43671b5e559", "createdAt": 1792407151208, "updatedAt": 1792407151208, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "SectionPlane.key includes enabled. Good.\n\nNow I have enough for the design. Let me write the checkpoint (this is around turn 8) and record design in scratchpad, then implement.\n\nDesign summary:\n\n**1. GPU hover coalescing (qt.py)**\n- `_InputSurface.mouseMoveEvent`: unpressed GPU moves now call `self.owner._queue_hover(e, interval=0)` instead of `_hover_select(e)` directly. Latest event wins (existing `_pending_hover` slot), single shared QTimer restarted at 0ms → one delivery per event-loop pass, stale canceled by existing `_cancel_hover` paths (press/wheel/drag/leave/hide/focus). `_deliver_hover` unchanged (checks surface identity, underMouse, drag, closed). Clicks unchanged (press path already cancels hover first).\n- Modify `_queue_hover(self, event, interval=120)` — software keeps 120ms; GPU passes 0.\n\n**2. Software retained preparation cache (qt.py)**\n- New private `_SoftwarePreparation` per (id(handle), chunk_id) stored on the viewer (`self._software_prep`), invalidated by signature: (handle.generations, mask revision for handle/chunk, section plane key, show_mesh_lines, highlight_fill, appearance scalar style (scalar_range, colormap), face_colors identity, outline_groups identity...). Prepared data: world array; admitted triangle indices + per-triangle color; per-triangle section-clipped world verts (when section enabled); world-space line segments post-clip + selected flags; point world positions + selected flags; outline loops (index tuples) via _surface_outline_loops with the same _outline_groups validation.\n- `_paint_software` uses prep for invariant work; projection (frame math), depth sort and painter submission unchanged → identical pixels.\n- _SoftwareResources gains `mask_revision` counter bumped by mask mutators.\n\nWait — one important check: `_paint_software` currently computes `appearance = dict(entry[\"appearance\"], scalar_range=..., colormap=...)` per chunk per frame — cheap. The heavy parts: world transform (matmul), the per-triangle Python loop with admission + color, per-line clipping loop, per-point loop, outline loop extraction. My prep caches all of those.\n\nBut careful about exact behavior preservation in the triangle loop: current code appends `faces.append((depth, screen, color, appearance))` where depth = mean of screen z. Screen per frame. Colors cached. The admission loop also builds `visible_triangles` list used for outlines. Prep caches admitted indices list; visible_triangles = prep triangles (same order).\n\nFor the section path per triangle: `verts = np.array([p.to_tuple() for p in section.clip_polygon(world[triangle])])` — cache verts array per admitted triangle (world-space); per frame `screen, visible = self._project_world_points(verts, frame)`. Identical.\n\nLines: current: `segment=(Point3D(*world[a]),Point3D(*world[b])); if self._section_plane is not None: segment=self._section_plane.clip_segment(*segment); if segment is None: continue; a,b=(self._project_point_with_frame(p, frame) for p in segment)`. Note: when section plane is None, segment stays Point3D pair. When plane exists (even disabled?), clip_segment is called. Does clip_segment check enabled? Let me check clipping.py clip_segment — need to verify. If clip_segment ignores enabled, then a disabled plane still clips lines — weird but must preserve. I'll cache the post-clip world segment endpoints as plain tuples and per frame call `self._project_point_with_frame(Point3D(*p), frame)`. _project_point_with_frame calls as_point(point) — accepts Point3D; passing Point3D constructed from cached tuples gives identical math.\n\nPoints: current: `if index in hidden_points or projected[i] is None: continue; if self._section_plane is not None and not self._section_plane.contains(world[i]): continue`. projected[i] per-frame; section contains cached (admission). Cache: list of (node_index, selected_flag) admitted; per frame check projected[node].\n\nOutlines: `if self.show_mesh_lines and (appearance.get(\"mesh_lines\") or entry.get(\"outline_groups\") is None): for screen in self._surface_outline_segments(...)`. _surface_outline_segments has camera-dependent fast/slow path selection. To preserve pixels exactly, keep calling _surface_outline_segments per frame but with cached... hmm, its camera-independent heavy part is `_surface_outline_loops(mesh, triangles, groups)` (boundary extraction over admitted triangles). I can add an optional `loops` parameter? _surface_outline_segments is in retained_viewer.py (owned). I'll refactor: `_surface_outline_segments(entry, chunk_id, mesh, triangles, world, frame=None, loops=None)` where loops, when provided, skips recomputation. The fast path and slow path both use loops. The final \"cut\" section iterates triangles with plane/depth — camera-dependent, keep.\n\nActually simpler: prep caches `loops = list(_surface_outline_loops(mesh, admitted, groups))` (after _outline_groups validation), passes loops=... into _surface_outline_segments. The function signature change is backward compatible (default None → compute).\n\nHmm wait, but _outline_groups validation mutates entry[\"outline_groups\"]=None if stale — do that during prep build.\n\nAlso the depth-range fast-path check inside _surface_outline_segments uses `depths.min() > frame.near and depths.max() < frame.far` — computed per frame from world. Fine.\n\n**3. Animation reuse (retained_viewer.py)**\n- `_show_animation_frame`: build per-item compatibility signature vs previous frame's items (stored in `self._animation_items` — list of item dicts from previous frame, or store signature on _animation_entries). If previous handles exist, count matches, and per-item signature matches → reuse handle: update positions/displacements/scalars/active/transform/deformation_scale/selected/visible/appearance via handle mutators; rebuild _animation_entries dicts with reused handle and new frame's semantics/owners. Else → existing full rebuild path.\n- Signature per item: (positions shape+array_equal? — expensive but ok; actually for reuse decision compare: positions array_equal, triangles array_equal, lines array_equal, point_indices array_equal, triangle_to_element array_equal-or-None, line_to_element, element_ids, active_elements shape? active is updatable... but active_elements length = element_count fixed by topology; values updatable via set_active_elements. element_scalars/node_scalars: updatable. So signature = topology fields equality + chunk ids + per-chunk topology equality + face_colors equality + appearance non-scalar-style keys equality? Appearance: color/opacity/etc. static per group; if frames differ in opacity, reuse would be stale (group style set at add_mesh). Include the filtered appearance kwargs (the add_mesh-accepted set minus scalar_range/colormap) in signature.\n- Also owners/owner_resolver/tags/item/layer: entry-level, rebuilt each frame from item — no staleness since we rebuild entries. But pick binding fallback uses id(handle) — stable under reuse. Owner tables come from item per frame — fine.\n\nWait, actually there's a question of whether reusing handles changes pick identity semantics in a way that breaks selection during animation. Previously each frame created new handles → new id(handle) → pick keys changed per frame. Selection during animation: query results reference handles... With reuse, keys stable across frames — better. \"preserve owner/pick identities\" — with owners tables, identity comes from owners, not handle id. OK.\n\nAlso must preserve: `_restore_live_renderer` removes animation handles — unchanged. `stop_animation` — unchanged. Incompatible frame → full rebuild (remove old handles from renderer, create new) — existing code pa