...). active: set_active_elements (allows None? `self._replace(\"active_elements\", values, \"active\")` — None fine presumably).\n\nBut careful: these mutators bump generations; renderer._sync compares group.generations (snapshot at last render) — detects changes. \n\nBut there's a catch: handle._mesh replacement via _replace keeps other fields — but between frames, multiple fields change; calling several mutators sequentially creates several MeshArrays copies — fine.\n\nAlso: the animation handle was created WITHOUT on_change callback — updates won't notify viewer. Good.\n\nBut wait — validation: handle._validate_mesh is None for animation handles. OK.\n\nEdge: what if item[\"mesh\"] arrays are identical objects across frames (owned_copy each capture → new objects). array_equal comparisons each frame: positions (N×3), triangles, etc. For 9712-triangle mesh: triangles 9712×3 uint32 ≈ 116KB compare — trivial. positions ~ maybe 5k×3 floats — trivial.\n\n**4. Selection key narrowing (retained_viewer.py)**\n- In `_selection_key`, replace `entry[\"handle\"].generations` with a narrowed tuple excluding scalar and appearance generations:\n```python\ngenerations = entry[\"handle\"].generations\n(generations.topology, generations.position, generations.displacement, generations.active, generations.selection, generations.transform)\n```\nKeep visible/removed/deformation_scale as-is. This preserves geometry/visibility/ownership (selection gen covers ownership changes) invalidation while scalar-only changes (scalar gen, appearance gen from update_appearance) no longer rebuild the projected index. Note set_visible bumps 'appearance' but visible is already in key.\n\n**5. Queued scheduler notification (scheduler.py + qt.py)**\n- scheduler.py: add `_notifier` (None default), `set_notifier(callback)`, `pending()` helper; submit() calls notifier after enqueue, guarded try/except (failure must not lose the queued item or crash the producer; record? keep silent-but-safe: swallow Exception). Hmm — \"failures safe\": swallow with no lost item; the item stays queued and will be drained by any later drain (e.g., redraw-triggered drains or next submit's notification). Also drain(): after the loop, if queue non-empty (limit reached), invoke notifier again to continue draining. close(): set notifier None first (avoid notifications during close), then closed=True, clear queue.\n  Wait — order: set _notifier=None then _closed=True then drain queue. Late submit raises RuntimeError (unchanged).\n- qt.py: replace `_start_update_pump` timer with `_UpdateNotifier(QObject)` + custom event posted via QCoreApplication.postEvent (thread-safe). `_poll_updates` stays (drain+redraw). The notifier's customEvent calls owner._poll_updates; if scheduler.pending() after drain, repost event (bounded 1024 per drain, continuation via repost → no lost items, no unbounded loop within one event).\n  Keep method name `_start_update_pump` (tests reference it) but change internals: no QTimer; set `self._update_poll_id = notifier` (guard). destroy(): close scheduler (existing) + notifier is child QObject → deleted with viewer; posted events to deleted objects discarded. Also set_notifier(None) on destroy before close? close() itself clears notifier.\n  \n  No QApplication at construction? QtViewer is a QWidget → app must exist. But the Pump fake test uses QCoreApplication — fine, postEvent works with QCoreApplication.\n\n  Initial drain before notifier creation (existing behavior: initial drain happens first so callback failure escapes constructor). Order: initial drain (may raise → no notifier created), then create notifier, set_notifier, then... items submitted between drain and set_notifier: window exists — a worker thread could submit in that window; after set_notifier there's no pending notification → lost wakeup until next submit. Fix: after set_notifier, drain again (and redraw if completed). I'll do: initial drain → create+set notifier → second drain (cheap, usually 0).\n\n  Hmm wait, but the initial-drain-failure test: `test_initial_callback_failure_does_not_leave_running_timer` expects `_update_poll_id is None` and no timers after failure, then restart works. With notifier: failure during initial drain → exception propagates, notifier never created, `_update_poll_id` None. Good. I'll update that test.\n\n  Also `test_one_timer_across_repeated_start_and_idle_ticks` asserts a 2ms QTimer — must be rewritten for the new contract (no timer; repeated start idempotent; no wakeups when idle). And `test_worker_submission_runs_FIFO_on_owner_through_real_timer` → through posted event. `test_drain_limit_retained_and_next_tick_finishes` → 1024 bound + repost continuation. `test_destroy_stops_timer...` → destroy closes scheduler, stale event discarded, late submit raises.\n\nNow — Tk compatibility: retained_viewer._poll_updates (Tk path) unchanged; scheduler default notifier None → submit doesn't notify. Tk hosts unaffected. Also `hosts.py` (Tk host) not owned — untouched.\n\nTest plan (new file tests/test_performance_improvements.py + updates to test_qt_update_pump.py, test_scheduler.py):\n1. Hover: GPU move coalescing — fake owner with backend_name 'gpu': queue multiple moves → one _hover_select with latest; press cancels; software still 120ms.\n2. Software prep cache: reuse counts (world builds once across camera moves), pixel-identical vs uncached across mutations (highlight, section, visibility, scalars, transform, deformation, active, selection, topology replace_chunk, masks), invalidation on each.\n3. Animation reuse: fake renderer counting add_mesh/remove_mesh; compatible frames reuse handles (no add_mesh), scalars/transform updates propagate via generations; incompatible frame (different triangles) rebuilds; stop_animation restores live; cancel; chunk incompatibility.\n4. Selection key: scalar-only change keeps index (no rebuild — count rebuilds via monkeypatched _projected_selection_index build or check _selection_index identity), while topology/position/transform/selection/visibility changes rebuild.\n5. Scheduler: notifier called on submit; cross-thread FIFO via posted event; 1024 bound + continuation; close safe; notifier failure doesn't lose item; Tk default no notifier.\n\nBudget: tests each ≤120s via C:/Python/Python314/python.exe -m pytest.\n\nNow write the checkpoint. The user said \"Return early checkpoint after ~8turns concrete choices/changes then finish\". I'll post the checkpoint as response text now (turn ~8), then continue implementing in following turns. Actually — should the checkpoint be a response message? \"Return early checkpoint\" — yes, an interim report in chat. But if I end my turn, the user must reply to continue... In this worker context, the parent expects me to continue to MaxTurns40. Hmm — \"Return early checkpoint after ~8turns concrete choices/changes then finish\" — I think this means: around turn 8, produce a checkpoint (in my response), then continue and finish within the turn budget. Since ending my turn waits for user input, and the instruction says \"then finish\", I should include the checkpoint as part of an ongoing response... but I can't emit a mid-response message and keep working without ending the turn.\n\nActually in this harness, each assistant message with tool calls is a \"turn\". I've used ~8 tool-call turns. The checkpoint should be visible to the parent — I'll write it to the response text of my next message(s) as I go. Best approach: save a checkpoint artifact to reports/performance-20261019/worker/checkpoint.md and mention it; continue working. The parent reads the final response; the checkpoint file preserves the mid-state. I'll write the checkpoint file now and continue.\n\nLet me write the checkpoint file and proceed to implementation.", "summary": []}
