{"id": "reasoning-1f118a40-4e56-54d4-9da5-b03cee375dbe-0", "sessionId": "0c772a71-d421-e6a8-14d9-dcb034248b0b", "turnId": "turn-2f430b3805d99f342897b43671b5e559", "createdAt": 1792407271985, "updatedAt": 1792407271985, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Now qt.py changes. Three areas:\n\nA. Hover (improvement 1): `_queue_hover(self, event, interval=120)`; mouseMoveEvent GPU branch → `self.owner._queue_hover(e, interval=0)`.\n\nB. Update pump → queued notification (improvement 5 Qt side):\n- Module-level: register event type. `from PySide6.QtCore import QEvent` — need import. `_UPDATE_EVENT_TYPE = QEvent.Type(QEvent.registerEventType())`.\n- Class `_UpdateNotifier(QObject)` with customEvent → owner._poll_updates() guarded.\n- `_start_update_pump` rewrite.\n\nCareful with the existing Pump fake test class — I'm rewriting that test anyway.\n\nImplementation in qt.py:\n\n```python\nfrom PySide6.QtCore import QCoreApplication, QEvent, QPointF, Qt, QTimer, Signal\n\n_UPDATE_EVENT_TYPE = QEvent.Type(QEvent.registerEventType())\n\nclass _UpdateEvent(QEvent):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__(_UPDATE_EVENT_TYPE)\n\nclass _UpdateNotifier(QObject):\n    \"\"\"Owner-thread wakeup fed by the shared scheduler's notifier hook.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, owner, parent=None):\n        super().__init__(parent)\n        self._owner = owner\n    def customEvent(self, event):\n        if event.type() is not _UPDATE_EVENT_TYPE:\n            return\n        owner = self._owner\n        if owner._closed:\n            return\n        try:\n            owner._poll_updates()\n        except Exception:\n            # A failing callback propagates out of drain(); the notifier\n            # stays armed so later submissions still wake the owner.\n            raise\n```\n\nHmm — should callback failure propagate through the event loop? In the timer pump, a failing callback propagated through timeout signal → Qt prints and continues? Actually in the old pump, `_poll_updates` → drain → callback raises → propagates through Qt signal invocation → Qt catches and prints (PySide6 swallows exceptions in slots? No — PySide6 prints traceback and continues, or aborts?). The old test `test_initial_callback_failure_does_not_leave_running_timer` only tests constructor-time failure. For the event path, if callback raises inside customEvent, PySide6 will print traceback to stderr and continue processing events — the notifier remains armed. That's \"failures safe\". I'll let it propagate (no try) to match Qt slot behavior... Actually safer to let it propagate naturally; Qt handles it. Keep customEvent simple without try.\n\n`_start_update_pump`:\n```python\ndef _start_update_pump(self):\n    if self._closed or self._update_poll_id is not None:\n        return\n    # Drain anything queued before the wakeup exists; a callback failure\n    # here escapes the constructor with no notifier left behind.\n    self._poll_updates()\n    if self._closed:\n        return\n    notifier = _UpdateNotifier(self, self)\n    self._update_poll_id = notifier\n    self._update_notifier = notifier\n    self._update_scheduler.set_notifier(\n        lambda: QCoreApplication.postEvent(notifier, _UpdateEvent()))\n    # A submission racing the setup above gets no wakeup; drain once more.\n    self._poll_updates()\n```\n\nWait — `_poll_updates` calls `self.redraw()` if completed. In constructor that's fine (old code did initial `_poll_updates` too).\n\nBut hold on: `_UpdateNotifier(self, self)` — parent self (QWidget). For the Pump fake (QObject), parent works too. But the fake Pump class doesn't define `_update_notifier`; I'll set attribute. Also destroy(): the notifier is a child QObject → deleted when viewer deleted. But `destroy()` calls `self._update_scheduler.close()` which detaches notifier. Also posted events pending after close: customEvent checks owner._closed → return. Good.\n\nAlso `_poll_updates` in QtViewer currently:\n```python\ndef _poll_updates(self):\n    if self._closed: return\n    completed = self._update_scheduler.drain()\n    if completed: self.redraw()\n```\nKeep. The 1024-bound continuation: drain() itself re-invokes notifier → posts another event → next event loop pass drains the remainder. Good — bounded per drain, no lost wakeup.\n\nOne issue: `QCoreApplication.postEvent` requires a QCoreApplication instance? postEvent works if instance exists; QtViewer requires QApplication anyway. In tests, fixture creates QCoreApplication. But `_UpdateEvent()` construction at module import — fine.\n\nAlso need to remove the QTimer import usage? QTimer still used elsewhere (hover, after()). Keep import.\n\nC. Software prep cache (improvement 2) — the big one. Add to qt.py:\n\n```python\nclass _SoftwareChunkPrep:\n    __slots__ = (\"signature\",\"world\",\"triangles\",\"colors\",\"section_polygons\",\n                \"line_segments\",\"line_selected\",\"points\",\"point_selected\",\"loops\",\n                \"scalar_limits\")\n```\n\nWhere:\n- world: np array (N,3)\n- triangles: list of admitted triangle indices (ints)\n- colors: list of color strings parallel to triangles\n- section_polygons: None or list of np arrays (world-space clipped verts) parallel to triangles (only when section enabled)\n- line_segments: list of ((ax,ay,az),(bx,by,bz)) tuples (world, post section clip)\n- line_selected: list of bool\n- points: list of (node_index, world_position tuple)? For projection we need projected[i] where i = node index — per frame we have screen_all/visible_all and projected list. Current code: `if index in hidden_points or projected[i] is None: continue` — index is the point ordinal, i is node index. Then `points.append((projected[i],style))`. So cache: list of (ordinal, node_index, selected). Per frame: `if projected[node] is None: continue; points.append((projected[node], style))` where style = dict(appearance) with point_color override if selected.\n- loops: outline loops (tuples of node indices) or None\n\nSignature per (handle, chunk_id):\n```python\n(handle.generations,\n mask_revision,  # renderer-wide revision; per-handle granularity: entry in masks dict changes... simplest: whole-revision\n section_key,    # None or plane.key\n show_mesh_lines,\n highlight_fill,\n appearance_style,  # (scalar_range, colormap) — from handle.appearance\n face_colors,   # identity/equality\n outline_groups_valid,  # after validation, entry[\"outline_groups\"] is None or its identity\n)\n```\n\nHmm — outline_groups: entry[\"outline_groups\"] may be mutated to None by validation. Include `id(entry[\"outline_groups\"])`? Arrays are np arrays; identity works since it's only replaced (set to None) not mutated. Use `None if entry.get(\"outline_groups\") is None else id(...)`? But the loops also depend on admitted triangles (covered by generations + mask revision) and mesh.triangles (topology gen).\n\nWait, subtle: `_outline_groups(entry, chunk_id, mesh)` checks `len(groups) != mesh.triangle_count or topology is not mesh.triangles and not array_equal(topology, mesh.triangles)` → sets entry[\"outline_groups\"]=None. mesh.triangles identity changes on topology change (gen bump). OK.\n\nAlso `appearance` dict: `dict(entry[\"appearance\"], scalar_range=..., colormap=...)` — the base dict keys (color, opacity, line_color, line_width, point_color, point_size, mesh_lines, invalid_color, face_colors...) are static per entry (set at add_mesh_arrays; only scalar_range/colormap mutated, covered by handle.appearance in signature). But wait — is entry[\"appearance\"] truly never mutated elsewhere? Let me grep for `[\"appearance\"]` mutations... `_mesh_changed` mutates scalar_range/colormap. `add_mesh_arrays` creates it. Anything else? Let me grep quickly during implementation.\n\nMask revision: add to _SoftwareResources:\n```python\nclass _SoftwareResources:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.revision = 0\n        ...\n    def add_mesh(...): self.revision += 1\n    def remove_mesh(...): self.revision += 1\n    def set_semantic_masks(...): self.revision += 1\n    def set_highlighted_elements(...): self.revision += 1\n    def set_chunk_semantic_masks(...): self.revision += 1\n    def clear_chunk_semantic_masks(...): self.revision += 1\n    def set_chunk_pickable(...): self.revision += 1  # pickable affects... chunk_pickable used in paint? Not in _paint_software. But bump anyway for safety.\n```\n_paint_software reads `self._renderer.masks` / `chunk_masks`. Revision bump on any mask mutation → conservative invalidation. Fine.\n\nNow the prep builder — replicate the current per-chunk invariant work exactly:\n\n```python\ndef _software_chunk_prep(self, entry, handle, chunk_id, mesh, section):\n    # returns _SoftwareChunkPrep\n    positions = mesh.positions\n    if mesh.displacements is not None:\n        positions = positions + handle.deformation_scale*mesh.displacements\n    world = positions @ handle.transform[:3,:3].T + handle.transform[:3,3]\n    mask = ... (same as current)\n    hidden/highlighted/preselected sets\n    colors = appearance.get(\"face_colors\")\n    scalars = ...\n    scalar_limits = ...\n    triangles = []; colors_out = []; section_polygons = [] if section else None\n    for index, triangle in enumerate(mesh.triangles):\n        element = ...\n        if active/hidden: continue\n        if section is not None:\n            verts = np.array([p.to_tuple() for p in section.clip_polygon(world[triangle])])\n            # current code computes this then checks len(screen)<3 after projection\n            section_polygons.append(verts)  # keep even short ones; per-frame check identical\n        color = ... (same logic)\n        triangles.append(index); colors_out.append(color)\n    lines: for index,(a,b) in enumerate(mesh.lines): if hidden: continue\n        segment=(Point3D(*world[a]),Point3D(*world[b]))\n        if self._section_plane is not None: segment=clip_segment; if None: continue\n        line_segments.append(((...a...),(...b...))); line_selected.append(index in selected_lines)\n    points: for index,i in enumerate(mesh.point_indices):\n        if index in hidden_points: continue\n        if self._section_plane is not None and not contains(world[i]): continue\n        points.append((index, int(i)))\n        point_selected.append(index in selected_points)\n    loops: if show_mesh_lines and (appearance.get(\"mesh_lines\") or entry.get(\"outline_groups\") is None):\n        groups = self._outline_groups(entry, chunk_id, mesh)  # validation side effect\n        loops = tuple(_surface_outline_loops(mesh, triangles, groups))\n    else: loops = None\n```\n\nWait — careful with the section triangle path: current code, when section is not None, does NOT check triangle_visible; it clips and projects. The admitted list in section mode: current code appends `visible_triangles.append(index)` AFTER the section check `if len(screen)<3 or not visible.all(): continue`. So in section mode, admitted triangles for outlines are only those with ≥3 visible clipped verts! My prep caches admission before projection (camera-dependent visibility). Hmm — in section mode, `visible_triangles` (used for outline loops) depends on camera visibility of clipped polygon. That breaks caching of loops in section mode.\n\nOptions: in section mode, don't cache loops; compute loops per frame from the per-frame visible_triangles list. The loops extraction is the expensive part though... but section mode is the slow path anyway (correctness first). So: prep caches triangles/colors/section_polygons; per frame, build visible_triangles from projection results (as now), and if loops needed, compute `_surface_outline_loops(mesh, visible_triangles, groups)` per frame in section mode. In non-section mode, admission is camera-independent → cache loops.\n\nWait, in non-section mode current code: `if not triangle_visible[index]: continue` — camera-dependent visibility! visible_triangles in non-section mode also depends on camera (all three verts visible). Ugh. So outline loops depend on camera-visible triangles in BOTH modes. The outline fast path in _surface_outline_segments projects only admitted (visible) triangles' boundaries.\n\nHmm. So loops can't be cached across camera frames... unless I check: `_surface_outline_loops(mesh, triangles, groups)` — boundary extraction over the visible set. Camera changes → visible set changes → loops change. So caching loops across camera motion is invalid!\n\nLet me reconsider: what's cacheable across camera changes:\n- world positions ✓\n- admission by active/hidden (camera-independent) — but visible_triangles (post-projection) is camera-dependent.\n- colors per admitted triangle ✓\n- section-clipped polygons (world) ✓\n- line segments (world, post-clip) ✓\n- point admission (hidden/section) ✓ (projected[i] check per frame)\n- outline loops: NOT cacheable across camera changes (depend on visible triangle set).\n\nBut `_surface_outline_loops` cost: it builds boundary edges from the admitted triangle set. Per frame it's recomputed — that's part of the current per-frame cost anyway. The plan says \"software camera frame avoid invariant work via properly invalidated retained preparation/cache\" — the invariant work is world transform, admission, colors, clipping. Loop extraction stays per-frame (it's camera-dependent). OK — simpler and correct.\n\nActually wait, let me check _surface_outline_loops signature: `_surface_outline_loops(mesh, triangles, groups)` at line 85. It takes the visible triangle list. Yes, per-frame.\n\nSo the prep cache:\n- world\n- admitted triangles (pre-visibility) + colors + section polygons\n- line segments + selected flags\n- point admissions + selected flags\n- scalar_limits (needed for colors — computed during prep)\n\nPer-frame _paint_software:\n```python\nframe = _FrameProjection(self)\nsection = ...\nfor entry in ...:\n    handle...\n    appearance = dict(entry[\"appearance\"], scalar_range=..., colormap=...)\n   