{"id": "reasoning-30adbd1a-437d-5341-a5b9-6d01d3739bbe-0", "sessionId": "0c772a71-d421-e6a8-14d9-dcb034248b0b", "turnId": "turn-2f430b3805d99f342897b43671b5e559", "createdAt": 1792407388250, "updatedAt": 1792407388250, "generationStatus": "completed", "relatedEntryId": null, "type": "reasoning", "text": "Now the mouseMoveEvent GPU hover change and _queue_hover interval parameter. Current code:\n\n```python\n    def mouseMoveEvent(self, event):\n        e = self._event(event)\n        if event.buttons() & Qt.LeftButton:\n            self.owner._cancel_hover()\n            self.owner._drag_select(e)\n        elif event.buttons():\n            self.owner._cancel_hover()\n            self.owner._motion(e)\n        elif self.owner.backend_name == \"software\":\n            self.owner._queue_hover(e)\n        else:\n            self.owner._hover_select(e)\n        event.accept()\n```\n\nChange `self.owner._hover_select(e)` → `self.owner._queue_hover(e, 0)`.\n\nAnd `_queue_hover(self, event)` → `_queue_hover(self, event, interval=120)` with `self._hover_timer.start(interval)`.\n\nAlso `_deliver_hover` — for GPU, the check `surface.underMouse()` etc. stays. But one more consideration: `_deliver_hover` checks `not self._drag and not self._selection_dragging` — good.\n\nAlso the hover timer for GPU: reuses same `_hover_timer` (single-shot). With interval 0, `timer.start(0)` restarts — coalesces bursts.\n\nNext: `_start_update_pump` rewrite. Current:\n\n```python\n    def _start_update_pump(self):\n        if self._closed or self._update_poll_id is not None:\n            return\n        self._poll_updates()\n        if self._closed:\n            return\n        # Reuse one Qt timer. The shared Tk implementation uses after(), which\n        # otherwise creates/deletes a QObject on every 2 ms poll in this host.\n        timer = QTimer(self)\n        self._update_poll_id = timer\n        self._timers.add(timer)\n        timer.timeout.connect(self._poll_updates)\n        timer.start(2)\n```\n\nNew:\n\n```python\n    def _start_update_pump(self):\n        if self._closed or self._update_poll_id is not None:\n            return\n        # Drain work that arrived before the wakeup existed; a callback\n        # failure here escapes the constructor with no notifier armed.\n        self._poll_updates()\n        if self._closed:\n            return\n        notifier = _UpdateNotifier(self, self)\n        self._update_notifier = notifier\n        self._update_poll_id = notifier\n        self._update_scheduler.set_notifier(\n            lambda: QCoreApplication.postEvent(notifier, _UpdateEvent()))\n        # A submission racing setup gets no wakeup; drain once more so it\n        # cannot wait for the next submit.\n        self._poll_updates()\n```\n\nWait — `_poll_updates` calls `self.redraw()` when completed>0. In the constructor, old code called `_poll_updates()` once (initial drain) — redraw during init was already possible. Now a second drain — same behavior. OK.\n\nHmm, but there's an issue: `_UpdateNotifier(self, self)` — parent `self` requires self to be a QObject — QtViewer is QWidget ✓; the fake Pump test class is QObject ✓ (I'll update the test).\n\nAlso `destroy()`: `attempt(\"close_scheduler\", self._update_scheduler.close)` — close() detaches notifier ✓. The notifier object itself: child of viewer, deleted with it. But also after destroy, `self._update_poll_id` still points to notifier; `_start_update_pump` after destroy → `self._closed` → return ✓.\n\nAlso `_poll_updates` — unchanged.\n\nNow `__init__` additions: `self._software_prep = {}` and `self._update_notifier = None`. Where? In QtViewer.__init__ before surface creation. Current init has `self._timers = set()` etc. Add after `self._pending_hover = None`:\n\n```python\n        self._software_prep = {}\n        self._update_notifier = None\n```\n\nNow the big one: `_paint_software` rewrite + prep builder. Let me write the new methods. I'll replace the whole `_paint_software` body and add `_software_chunk_prep` + `_software_prep_signature` methods before it.\n\nNew code (CRLF). Let me draft carefully, preserving exact per-frame math:\n\n```python\n    def _software_prep_signature(self, entry, handle, chunk_id):\n        appearance = entry[\"appearance\"]\n        return (\n            handle.generations,\n            getattr(self._renderer, \"mask_revision\", 0),\n            None if self._section_plane is None else self._section_plane.key,\n            self._highlight_fill,\n            handle.appearance.scalar_range,\n            handle.appearance.colormap,\n            appearance.get(\"color\"),\n            appearance.get(\"face_colors\"),\n            appearance.get(\"invalid_color\"),\n        )\n```\n\nWait — handle.appearance.colormap may be unhashable (colormap object)? It goes into a tuple key compared by == — needs equality comparable, not hashable (we compare tuples with ==, not hash). Tuple == compares elementwise with ==... actually tuple __eq__ first checks identity then ==. Colormap objects — appearance.py validated_appearance; colormap could be a string or object with __eq__. The existing `_selection_key` uses similar tuples. Risk: numpy arrays in signature → tuple == works elementwise? np.array == np.array returns array → bool(array) ambiguity! face_colors is a Sequence[str] (tuple of strings) per add_mesh — `appearance.get(\"face_colors\")` is a tuple of strings ✓. handle.appearance.scalar_range — tuple of floats ✓. colormap — could be arbitrary object; if it's a numpy array, == would break. validated_appearance probably normalizes colormap to a string or keeps object. The existing _selection_key doesn't include colormap. Hmm. To be safe, use identity for colormap: `id(handle.appearance.colormap)`? But an equal-but-different colormap object would invalidate → just a cache miss, safe. But if colormap is mutated in place (unlikely — validated_appearance creates new), id stays → stale. validated_appearance returns MeshAppearance frozen dataclass presumably. Use id() for colormap — safe both ways? If colormap is a mutable object mutated in place, id unchanged → stale cache. But then handle.generations.appearance bumps on update_appearance (which creates new MeshAppearance via validated_appearance — new colormap object or same?). If update_appearance passes the same colormap object, candidate==old → no change. OK, id() is acceptable; but simpler: keep colormap in the tuple but only if it's safely comparable... I'll use id() for the colormap to avoid __eq__ surprises.\n\nHmm wait, actually there's a subtle issue with `handle.generations` in the signature: DirtyGenerations is a frozen dataclass with eq — comparing two DirtyGenerations tuples is fine.\n\nNow the prep builder:\n\n```python\n    def _software_chunk_prep(self, entry, handle, chunk_id, mesh, section):\n        signature = self._software_prep_signature(entry, handle, chunk_id)\n        cache = self._software_prep\n        key = (id(handle), chunk_id)\n        prep = cache.get(key)\n        if prep is not None and prep.handle is handle and prep.signature == signature:\n            return prep\n        appearance = dict(entry[\"appearance\"], scalar_range=handle.appearance.scalar_range, colormap=handle.appearance.colormap)\n        positions = mesh.positions\n        if mesh.displacements is not None:\n            positions = positions + handle.deformation_scale*mesh.displacements\n        world = positions @ handle.transform[:3,:3].T + handle.transform[:3,3]\n        mask = self._renderer.masks.get(id(handle), {}) if chunk_id is None else self._renderer.chunk_masks.get((id(handle),chunk_id), {})\n        hidden = set(mask.get(\"hidden_elements\", ()))\n        highlighted = set(mask.get(\"selected_elements\", ())) | set(handle.selected_elements)\n        preselected = set(mask.get(\"preselected_elements\", ()))\n        colors = appearance.get(\"face_colors\")\n        scalars = mesh.element_scalars if mesh.element_scalars is not None else mesh.node_scalars\n        scalar_limits = None\n        if scalars is not None and not colors:\n            finite = scalars[np.isfinite(scalars)]\n            scalar_limits = (appearance.get(\"scalar_range\") or (float(finite.min()),float(finite.max()))) if len(finite) else (0.,1.)\n        triangles, triangle_colors = [], []\n        section_polygons = [] if section is not None else None\n        for index, triangle in enumerate(mesh.triangles):\n            element = int(mesh.triangle_to_element[index]) if mesh.triangle_to_element is not None else index\n            if mesh.active_elements is not None and not mesh.active_elements[element]:\n                continue\n            if element in hidden:\n                continue\n            if section is not None:\n                section_polygons.append(np.array([p.to_tuple() for p in section.clip_polygon(world[triangle])]))\n            color = self._highlight_fill if element in highlighted else (colors[index if len(colors)==mesh.triangle_count else element] if colors else appearance[\"color\"])\n            if scalars is not None and not colors and element not in highlighted:\n                value=float(scalars[element]) if mesh.element_scalars is not None else float(np.mean(scalars[triangle]))\n                color=scalar_color(value,*scalar_limits,appearance.get('colormap')) if np.isfinite(value) else appearance[\"invalid_color\"]\n            if element in preselected and element not in highlighted:color=\"#ffd166\"\n            triangles.append(index)\n            triangle_colors.append(color)\n        line_segments, line_selected = [], []\n        if mesh.lines is not None and not appearance.get(\"mesh_lines\"):\n            hidden_lines=set(mask.get(\"hidden_lines\",()))\n            selected_lines=set(mask.get(\"selected_lines\",()))\n            for index,(a,b) in enumerate(mesh.lines):\n                if index in hidden_lines:continue\n                segment=(Point3D(*world[a]),Point3D(*world[b]))\n                if self._section_plane is not None:segment=self._section_plane.clip_segment(*segment)\n                if segment is None:continue\n                line_segments.append((tuple(segment[0].to_tuple()), tuple(segment[1].to_tuple())))\n                line_selected.append(index in selected_lines)\n        points, point_selected = [], []\n        if mesh.point_indices is not None:\n            hidden_points=set(mask.get(\"hidden_points\",()))\n            selected_points=set(mask.get(\"selected_points\",()))\n            for index,i in enumerate(mesh.point_indices):\n                if index in hidden_points:continue\n                if self._section_plane is not None and not self._section_plane.contains(world[i]):continue\n                points.append(int(i))\n                point_selected.append(index in selected_points)\n        prep = _SoftwareChunkPrep(handle, signature, world, triangles, triangle_colors, section_polygons, line_segments, line_selected, points, point_selected)\n        cache[key] = prep\n        return prep\n```\n\nWait — careful with the line loop condition: current code has `if mesh.lines is not None and not appearance.get(\"mesh_lines\"):` — appearance here is the merged dict. In prep I use the same merged appearance. ✓\n\nPoint3D.to_tuple — Point3D has to_tuple? It's used elsewhere: `p.to_tuple()`. clip_segment returns Point3D pair. ✓\n\nOne issue: `colors[index if len(colors)==mesh.triangle_count else element]` — colors could be None; guarded by `if colors else appearance[\"color\"]` in the conditional expression. In my rewrite: `(colors[index if len(colors)==mesh.triangle_count else element] if colors else appearance[\"color\"])` ✓.\n\nAlso `appearance[\"color\"]` — key must exist (add_mesh_arrays always sets color). ✓\n\nNow the new `_paint_software`:\n\n```python\n    def _paint_software(self, painter):\n        painter.setRenderHint(QPainter.Antialiasing)\n        ratio = self.surface.devicePixelRatioF()\n        painter.scale(1/ratio, 1/ratio)\n        width, height = self.viewport_size\n        painter.fillRect(0, 0, width, height, QColor(self.bg))\n        # One camera basis/viewport/scale evaluation serves every projection\n        # in the frame; per-primitive work then only performs the math itself.\n        frame = _FrameProjection(self)\n        section = self._section_plane if self._section_plane is not None and self._section_plane.enabled else None\n        faces, lines, points, outlines = [], [], [], []\n        for entry in self._display_entries().values():\n            handle = entry[\"handle\"]\n            if handle.removed or not handle.visible:\n                continue\n            appearance = dict(entry[\"appearance\"],scalar_range=handle.appearance.scalar_range,colormap=handle.appearance.colormap)\n            if appearance.get(\"depth_only\"):\n                continue\n            for chunk_id, mesh in [(None, handle.mesh), *handle.chunks]:\n                # Camera-invariant preparation is retained across frames and\n                # invalidated by the signature above; only projection and\n                # submission remain per camera frame.\n                prep = self._software_chunk_prep(entry, handle, chunk_id, mesh, section)\n                world = prep.world\n                screen_all, visible_all = self._project_world_points(world, frame)\n                projected = [tuple(screen_all[i]) if visible_all[i] else None for i in range(len(screen_all))]\n                triangle_visible = None\n                if section is None and len(mesh.triangles):\n                    triangle_visible = visible_all[np.asarray(mesh.triangles)].all(axis=1)\n                visible_triangles = []\n                for position, index in enumerate(prep.triangles):\n                    if section is not None:\n                        verts = prep.section_polygons[position]\n                        screen, visible = self._project_world_points(verts, frame)\n                        if len(screen)<3 or not visible.all():\n                            continue\n                    else:\n                        if not triangle_visible[index]:\n                            continue\n                        screen = screen_all[mesh.triangles[index]]\n                    visible_triangles.append(index)\n                    faces.append((sum(p[2] for p in screen)/len(screen), screen, prep.colors[position], appearance))\n                if self.show_mesh_lines and (appearance.get(\"mesh_lines\") or entry.get(\"outline_groups\") is None):\n                    for screen in self._surface_outline_segments(entry, chunk_id, mesh, visible_triangles, world, frame):\n                        outlines.append((sum(point[2] for point in screen) / len(screen), screen, appearance))\n                for segment, selected in zip(prep.line_segments, prep.line_selected):\n                    a,b=(self._project_point_with_frame(Point3D(*p), frame) for p in segment)\n                    if a is not None and b is not None:\n                        style=dict(appearance)\n                        if selected:style[\"line_color\"]=self._highlight_fill\n                        lines.append((a,b,style))\n                for node, selected in zip(prep.points, prep.point_selected):\n                    if projected[node] is None:continue\n                    style=dict(appearance)\n                    if selected:style[\"point_color\"]=self._highlight_fill\n                    points.append((projected[node],style))\n        ... (rest unchanged: surfaces sort, painting, HUD)\n```\n\nWait — one behavioral check on the section triangle path: current code computes `verts` INSIDE the loop after admission checks, and the `if len(screen)<3 or not visible.all(): continue` happens before appending to visible_triangles and faces. My version: prep caches verts for admitted triangles; per frame the same checks. ✓ Order of faces: same. ✓\n\nAnother check — current code in se